QB.add(60,[{"n":1,"q":"Stroke patient presents with inability to identify objects but can identify the color, texture, shape of objects. Which of the following lobes is involved?","o":[{"t":"Frontal lobe"},{"t":"Parietal lobe"},{"t":"Temporal lobe"},{"t":"Occipital lobe"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Temporal lobe:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Patient history is suggestive of visual agnosia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A patient with visual agnosia is unable to either name a visually presented object or describe its use. Face and object recognition disorders also can result from simultanagnosia as seen in patients with Bálint's syndrome, in which case they are known as apperceptive agnosias as opposed to the associative agnosias that result from inferior temporal lobe lesions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Frontal lobe: Lesions in a patient's frontal lobe can lead to certain symptoms such as trouble with learning, deficits in visual-motor function, executive dysfunction and problems with attention (planning, focusing, and inhibition)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Parietal lobe: Parietal Lobe, Right - Damage to this area can cause visuospatial deficits (e.g., the patient may have difficulty finding their way around new, or even familiar, places). Parietal Lobe, Left - Damage to this area may disrupt a person's ability to understand spoken and/or written language."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Occipital lobe: Unilateral occipital lobe lesion causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia. It is a visual field defect on the same side of both eyes contralateral to the site of the lesion - lesions of the occipital lobe due to the posterior cerebral artery infarct cause homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing."}]},{"n":2,"q":"30-year-old patient presents with high grade fever for the past week. On examination neck stiffness and kernig's sign was positive. CSF analysis showed lymphocytosis protein – 80 mg/dl, glucose -20 mg/dl while RBS = 140 mg/dl. What is your diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Tubercular meningitis"},{"t":"Coxsackie meningitis"},{"t":"Bacterial meningitis"},{"t":"HSV -1 encephalitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Tubercular meningitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF sugar/ Random Blood sugar is noted to be (40/140) 0.28 with elevated CSF protein."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This along with CSF lymphocytosis favors a diagnosis of Tubercular meningitis ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options B and D: Coxsackie meningitis and HSV -1 encephalitis: These are ruled out as CSF sugar is normal in viral meningitis/encephalitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Bacterial meningitis: Bacterial meningitis is characterized by the presence of PMN Cells, therefore it can be ruled out."}]},{"n":3,"q":"30 year old HIV positive man presents with neck rigidity and papilledema. CSF sample showed a capsulated organism positive for India ink staining. Which of the following statements is true?","o":[{"t":"Intracranial pressure remains normal."},{"t":"Delay antiretroviral therapy by 4-6 weeks after the initiation of antifungal treatment."},{"t":"Initiate treatment with oral fluconazole."},{"t":"Brain imaging shows changes in > 80 % of patients."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Delay antiretroviral therapy by 4-6 weeks after the initiation of antifungal treatment:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Due to the risk of IRIS (immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome), ART should be deferred by 4-6 weeks after the initiation of antifungal treatment."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Intracranial pressure remains normal: It is unlikely that intracranial pressure is normal since the patient has papilledema on examination."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Initiate treatment with oral fluconazole: Fluconazole alone is not used to manage Cryptococcal meningitis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Consolidation: Fluconazole 400 mg/day for 8-10 days until CSF is sterile"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Maintenance: Fluconazole 200 mg/day lifelong"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Brain imaging shows changes in > 80 % of patients: Brain imaging shows changes in > 80 % of patients. It is incorrect as brain imaging showing meningoencephalitis is seen in less than 30 percent of cases and classical soap bubble appearance is not commonly seen as well."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 60 year old man present with sudden onset of right sided hemiparesis for last 3 hours. BP is 200/100 mmHg. NCCT Head showed brain bleed and right hemiparesis. All of the following are to be done for management, Except?","o":[{"t":"Thrombolysis"},{"t":"BP Control"},{"t":"Edema management"},{"t":"Elective hyperventilation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Thrombolysis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombolysis, also known as clot-busting therapy, involves administering medications to dissolve blood clots. It is typically used in the treatment of ischemic strokes (caused by a blood clot blocking a blood vessel in the brain). However, in this case, the patient's presentation and brain imaging findings suggest a hemorrhagic stroke, which is characterized by bleeding into the brain tissue. Administering thrombolytic therapy in a hemorrhagic stroke could exacerbate the bleeding and worsen the patient's condition, which is why thrombolysis is contraindicated in this scenario."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - BP Control: High blood pressure (hypertension) is a risk factor for both ischemic and hemorrhagic strokes. In the context of a hemorrhagic stroke, controlling blood pressure is crucial to prevent further bleeding and manage the patient's overall condition. So, BP control is anessential part of the management."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Edema Management: Brain edema (swelling) can occur following a stroke, and it can lead to increased intracranial pressure, potentially causing further damage. Edema management might involve measures such as elevating the head of the bed, using osmotic agents, and closely monitoring the patient's neurological status. This is an important aspect of stroke management."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Elective Hyperventilation: Elective hyperventilation, which involves intentionally increasing breathing rate, can lead to a decrease in carbon dioxide levels in the blood. This can lead to constriction of blood vessels in the brain (vasoconstriction) and reduction of cerebral blood flow. While it was previously used in the management of elevated intracranial pressure, it is generally not recommended due to the risk of worsening cerebral ischemia. Instead, other methods for intracranial pressure control are preferred."}],"qi":[8471]},{"n":5,"q":"A 60-year-old patient weighing 80 kilograms has been diagnosed with an acute ischemic stroke and is recommended for thrombolysis with alteplase. What should the initial stat dose in milligrams be?","o":[{"t":"8"},{"t":"10"},{"t":"12"},{"t":"15"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - 8"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Streptokinase is always given in the form of an infusion over 1 hour but alteplase, some part of it can be given as a bolus shot and the remaining can be given as infusion. 10% of the total dose need to be given in the form of a bolus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Calculation of the Total Dose:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The standard dose of alteplase for acute ischemic stroke is 0.9 mg per kilogram of body weight, with a maximum total dose of 90 mg."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For an 80 kg patient: Total dose=80 kg×0.9 mg/kg=72 mg"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Administration of the Dose:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bolus Dose: 10% of the total dose is administered as an initial bolus over 1 minute. Bolus dose=10%×72 mg=7.2 mg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bolus dose=10%×72 mg=7.2 mg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infusion Dose: The remaining 90% of the total dose is administered as an intravenous infusion over 60 minutes. Infusion dose=90%×72 mg=64.8 mg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infusion dose=90%×72 mg=64.8 mg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bolus dose=10%×72 mg=7.2 mg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infusion dose=90%×72 mg=64.8 mg"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Since the maximum dose of alteplase is 90 mg, and the calculated dose is 72 mg, the patient will receive the calculated dose of 72 mg. The 10 % of this will be 7.2 and the closest option is 8 so the answer is Option A"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - 10"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - 12"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - 15"}]},{"n":6,"q":"A 50-year-old man presents with movement disorder and tremors in hand, along with the development of cognitive and emotional problems. Which of the following medical disorders is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Parkinson's disease"},{"t":"Frederich's ataxia"},{"t":"Alzheimer’s disease"},{"t":"Huntington's disease"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Huntington's disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Huntington's disease is a genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the HTT gene, leading to the accumulation of abnormal protein in the brain. It presents with movement problems (chorea, which are involuntary, random, and brief movements), cognitive decline, and emotional disturbances such as depression and anxiety. The onset usually occurs in adulthood, typically between the ages of 30 and 50. The mutation involves a trinucleotide repeat expansion (CAG repeats) on the HTT gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Parkinson's disease: While Parkinson's disease can present with movement disorders and tremors, it typically develops in older individuals (usually after the age of 60). Additionally, cognitive and emotional problems are more characteristic of other conditions like Lewy body dementia, which can be associated with parkinsonism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Friedreich's ataxia: Friedreich's ataxia is a genetic disorder that primarily affects the nervous system, causing progressive damage to the spinal cord and peripheral nerves. It is characterized by problems with coordination, balance, and muscle control. While it can lead to movement problems, cognitive and emotional issues are not prominent features of this disorder."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Alzheimer's disease: Alzheimer's disease primarily affects memory, thinking, and behavior. It does not typically present with movement disorders and tremors. Cognitive and emotional problems are central to Alzheimer's, but the movement disorder symptoms described (tremors, chorea) are not characteristic of this disorder."}]},{"n":7,"q":"65-year-old man is brought to the hospital with complaints of suddenly not making any sense while speaking using non-sensical grammar though he can speak quickly. He could communicate effectively prior to this. Which of the following defect is present in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Motor aphasia"},{"t":"Anomic aphasia"},{"t":"Fluent aphasia"},{"t":"Conduction aphasia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Fluent aphasia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluent aphasia, also known as Wernicke's aphasia, is characterized by fluent and rapid speech, but the speech often lacks meaningful content. People with fluent aphasia produce long and grammatically complex sentences that might be devoid of actual meaning or might include neologisms (made-up words). They typically have difficulty understanding language and may not realize that their speech is nonsensical."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Motor aphasia: Motor aphasia, also known as Broca's aphasia, is characterized by difficulty in producing speech, with limited vocabulary, slow speech, and impaired grammar. People with motor aphasia struggle to form coherent sentences and might experience frustration due to their communication difficulties."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Anomic aphasia: Anomic aphasia is characterized by word-finding difficulties. People with this type of aphasia have trouble recalling specific words, which leads to pauses and substitutions in their speech. However, their grammar and fluency are relatively intact."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Conduction aphasia: Conduction aphasia is characterized by difficulty in repeating words and phrases, even though comprehension and spontaneous speech might be relatively intact. People with conduction aphasia have trouble bridging the gap between understanding and speaking, leading to errors in repeating what they have just heard."}]},{"n":8,"q":"Scissoring gait will occur due to increases firing of Which of the following nerve?","o":[{"t":"Superior gluteal nerve"},{"t":"Inferior gluteal nerve"},{"t":"Obturator nerve"},{"t":"Femoral nerve"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Obturator Nerve:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The obturator nerve innervates the muscles of the medial thigh, including the adductors (adductor magnus, adductor longus, adductor brevis, gracilis, and obturator externus)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased firing or overactivity of the adductor muscles innervated by the obturator nerve canresult in a scissoring gait. A scissoring gait is characterized by the legs crossing over eachother during walking due to excessive adduction of the hip and thigh muscles."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct A - Superior Gluteal Nerve: The superior gluteal nerve primarily innervates the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus muscles. Increased firing of this nerve would lead to weakness or overactivity of these muscles, resulting in a Trendelenburg gait (characterized by excessive lateral pelvic tilt and hip drop on the unsupported side during the stance phase of gait), not a scissoring gait."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Inferior Gluteal Nerve: The inferior gluteal nerve innervates the gluteus maximus muscle. An increase in firing of this nerve would affect the function of the gluteus maximus, potentially leading to altered hip extension, but it wouldn't directly cause a scissoring gait."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Femoral Nerve: The femoral nerve innervates the muscles of the anterior thigh, including the quadriceps femoris muscle group. Increased firing of the femoral nerve and subsequent overactivity of the quadriceps muscles might affect knee extension, but it wouldn't directly lead to a scissoring gait."}]},{"n":9,"q":"Antibody seen in Acute Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy will belong to which class?","o":[{"t":"Ig G"},{"t":"Ig M"},{"t":"Ig E"},{"t":"Ig A"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - (IgG):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy (AIDP) is commonly known as Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS). GBS is an autoimmune disorder where the immune system mistakenly attacks the peripheral nervous system, leading to inflammation and demyelination of nerves. In the context of GBS, the antibodies produced against components of peripheral nerves belong primarily to the IgG class."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IgG is the correct answer because IgG antibodies are the most involved immunoglobulins in GBS. These antibodies target myelin, which is the protective covering of nerve fibers, leading to impaired nerve conduction and the characteristic symptoms of muscle weakness, tingling, and paralysis seen in GBS patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - IgM: While IgM antibodies can be involved in certain autoimmune diseases, they are not typically associated with GBS. IgM antibodies are usually associated with early immune responses and are not as commonly found in autoimmune disorders affecting the nervoussystem."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - IgE: IgE antibodies are primarily associated with allergic reactions and parasitic infections. They are not a characteristic component of the immune response seen in GBS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - IgA: IgA antibodies are mainly found in mucosal secretions and play a role in defending mucous membranes. They are not typically associated with the immune response involved in GBS."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 45-year-old male presents to the outpatient department with complaints of daytime sleepiness and nighttime sleeplessness. His wife reports that he snores at night. Which of the following statements about his condition is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"The tone of the pharyngeal muscles is increased"},{"t":"Low oxygen saturation triggers waking up at night"},{"t":"Episodes of apnea increase stress on the heart"},{"t":"Low oxygen stimulates respiratory effort"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - The tone of the pharyngeal muscles is increased:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The scenario depicts obstructive sleep apnea (OSA). In individuals with a collapsible airway, reduced muscle tone leads to brief pharyngeal collapse (apneas) or near-collapse (hypopneas). These episodes typically end with ventilatory reflexes prompting arousal, increasing muscle activity, and reopening the airway. Collapse can occur at various sites, including the soft palate (most common), tongue base, lateral pharyngeal walls, and/or epiglottis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Low oxygen saturation triggers waking up at night: Apneic episodes are related to stress on the heart and waking up at night."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Episodes of apnea increase stress on the heart: Intensified snoring can lead to apneic episodes, stressing the heart. Rapid fluctuations in heart rate due to hypoxia induce peripheral vasoconstriction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Low oxygen stimulates respiratory effort: Hypoxia is the trigger that stimulates respiratory effort in a patient with obstructive sleep apnea."}]},{"n":11,"q":"All of the following is a risk factor for secondary headache and need further workup except?","o":[{"t":"50 years old with first severe headache"},{"t":"Presence of cord-like vessels in the temporal area"},{"t":"Cocaine abuse"},{"t":"Subacute worsening of headache symptoms"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cocaine abuse:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cocaine usage is not a factor of concern as cocaine itself contributes to the development of headaches as it causes vasoconstriction and increases blood pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - 50 years old with first severe headache: In an elderly new onset headache is a cause of concern and needs further investigation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Presence of cord-like vessels in the temporal area: Cord-like vessels are a feature of giant cell arteritis. Hence, the presence of cord-like vessels in the temporal area is a risk factor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Subacute worsening of headache symptoms: Subacute worsening of headache symptoms is suggestive of intracranial space-occupying lesions. Therefore, it is a risk factor for secondary headaches."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 16-year-old girl presents with generalized muscle weakness and protrusion of the eyelids (proptosis) that worsens as the day progresses. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Myasthenia Gravis"},{"t":"Fibromyalgia"},{"t":"Myotonic Dystrophy"},{"t":"Guillain-Barré Syndrome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Myasthenia Gravis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This autoimmune disorder is characterized by weakness of the skeletal muscles, which tends to worsen with activity and improve with rest. The muscle weakness often affects the eyelids (leading to ptosis or proptosis) and can become more pronounced as the day progresses."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Fibromyalgia: Fibromyalgia is a chronic condition characterized by widespread musculoskeletal pain, fatigue, and tenderness in localized areas. It does not typically present with the specific symptoms of muscle weakness and eyelid proptosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Myotonic Dystrophy: This genetic disorder involves progressive muscle wasting and weakness, with symptoms that can include muscle stiffness. However, it is less likely to present with the acute onset of muscle weakness and eyelid involvement described in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Guillain-Barré Syndrome: This is an acute, rapidly progressing autoimmune condition affecting the peripheral nervous system, leading to muscle weakness and sometimes paralysis. It does not typically present with isolated eyelid proptosis or a pattern of worsening weakness throughout the day."}]},{"n":13,"q":"Patient presents with fever, headache, and vomiting that have persisted for the past 2 weeks. On examination, nuchal rigidity is noted. The results of a lumbar puncture (LP) are as follows: Opening Pressure: Elevated Cells: Predominantly polymorphonuclear leukocytes (PMNs) Sugar: 35 mg/dl Protein: 200 mg/dl Color: Slightly cloudy appearance Based on these findings, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Meningoencephalitis"},{"t":"Viral Meningitis"},{"t":"Tuberculous (TB) Meningitis"},{"t":"Bacterial Meningitis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Bacterial Meningitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation and CSF findings are characteristic of bacterial meningitis. The elevated opening pressure, predominantly PMNs (polymorphonuclear leukocytes), low glucose level (35 mg/dl), and elevated protein level (200 mg/dl) are indicative of bacterial infection. Bacterial meningitis commonly presents with these features and requires prompt antibiotic treatment."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Meningoencephalitis: This condition involves inflammation of both the meninges and the brain parenchyma. It can present with similar symptoms, but the CSF profile described (especially the predominance of PMNs) is more typical of bacterial meningitis rather than meningoencephalitis, which may present with a more varied CSF profile."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Viral Meningitis: Viral or aseptic meningitis usually presents with a different CSF profile: a lower white cell count, often with a lymphocytic predominance rather than PMN predominance. Glucose levels are usually normal, and protein levels are elevated but typically not as high as in bacterial meningitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tuberculous (TB) Meningitis : TB meningitis typically presents with a more chronic course and a CSF profile with lymphocytic predominance, lower glucose levels, and often a higher protein concentration. The CSF is usually more clear or slightly cloudy rather than markedly cloudy, and the protein level may be elevated but not as high as in bacterial meningitis."}]},{"n":14,"q":"A patient with type 2 diabetes mellitus experiences hypoglycemia and sustains a fall. Due to incoherent speech, a CT scan is performed and reveals a substantial subdural hematoma (SDH) with a volume greater than 30 cc. What should be the immediate next step in management?","o":[{"t":"Administer Mannitol"},{"t":"Administer Aspirin and Clopidogrel"},{"t":"Perform a Craniotomy"},{"t":"Stabilize the patient and urgently seek a neurosurgical consultation"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Perform a Craniotomy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subdural Hematoma (SDH): This condition involves bleeding between the dura mater and the brain, often due to tearing of bridging veins."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT Imaging Characteristics: Acute SDH: Appears hyperdense (white) on CT. Chronic SDH: Appears hypodense (black) as blood is reabsorbed. Subdural Hematoma Volume: A large hematoma (>30 cc) with a low GCS score indicates a significant risk of brain compression and herniation, necessitating surgical intervention."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute SDH: Appears hyperdense (white) on CT."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic SDH: Appears hypodense (black) as blood is reabsorbed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subdural Hematoma Volume: A large hematoma (>30 cc) with a low GCS score indicates a significant risk of brain compression and herniation, necessitating surgical intervention."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Craniotomy is the recommended procedure for immediate surgical evacuation of a large SDH to relieve pressure and prevent further brain damage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute SDH: Appears hyperdense (white) on CT."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic SDH: Appears hypodense (black) as blood is reabsorbed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subdural Hematoma Volume: A large hematoma (>30 cc) with a low GCS score indicates a significant risk of brain compression and herniation, necessitating surgical intervention."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Administer Mannitol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mannitol is used to manage elevated intracranial pressure, but it is not the first-line treatment for a large subdural hematoma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Administer Aspirin and Clopidogrel"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aspirin and Clopidogrel are antiplatelet agents used to prevent thrombotic events but are contraindicated in the management of a subdural hematoma, particularly in an acute setting where bleeding is ongoing."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Stabilize the patient and urgently seek a neurosurgical consultation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While stabilizing the patient and obtaining a neurosurgical consultation are important, immediate surgical intervention is required given the large volume of the hematoma and the patient's clinical condition."}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with ptosis, diplopia, and generalized muscle weakness that worsens with activity and improves with rest. She has difficulty chewing and swallowing, especially in the evening. A Tensilon test is positive. Which investigation is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"CT scan of the chest"},{"t":"SFEMG"},{"t":"Anti-acetylcholine receptor antibody test"},{"t":"MRI of the brain"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - SFEMG:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms worsening with activity and improving with rest clearly indicate Myasthenia Gravis (MG)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SFEMG is the most sensitive test for diagnosing MG. It detects abnormalities in the neuromuscular transmission by measuring the electrical activity between pairs of muscle fibers innervated by the same motor axon. SFEMG is considered the gold standard and the investigation of choice for confirming MG."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms worsening with activity and improving with rest clearly indicate Myasthenia Gravis (MG)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"SFEMG is the most sensitive test for diagnosing MG. It detects abnormalities in the neuromuscular transmission by measuring the electrical activity between pairs of muscle fibers innervated by the same motor axon. SFEMG is considered the gold standard and the investigation of choice for confirming MG."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - CT scan of the chest: A CT scan of the chest is useful for evaluating the thymus gland for abnormalities such as thymoma, which is associated with MG. However, it is not the primary diagnostic tool for confirming MG."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Anti-acetylcholine receptor antibody test: This test checks for the presence of antibodies against acetylcholine receptors, which are found in approximately 85% of generalized MG cases and 50% of ocular MG cases. While it is specific and highly indicative of MG, SFEMG is more sensitive."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - MRI of the brain: An MRI of the brain can help rule out other neurological conditions that might present with similar symptoms, such as multiple sclerosis or brainstem lesions. However, it is not useful for diagnosing MG specifically."}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 30-year-old man experiences initial paralysis in both legs, which then progresses to affect both upper limbs as well. Over time, he shows improvement but is left with paralysis affecting only his lower trunk and both legs. Based on this description, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Diplegia"},{"t":"Quadriplegia"},{"t":"Paraplegia"},{"t":"Monoplegia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Diplegia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is characterized by paralysis that primarily affects symmetrical parts of the body, often the legs more than the arms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the man initially had paralysis in both legs and then developed upper limb involvement, but ultimately his condition improved to show significant paralysis in the lower trunk and legs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The description fits diplegia as it involves predominant paralysis of the lower body while sparing or minimally affecting the upper limbs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Quadriplegia : This involves paralysis of all four limbs and typically includes the torso. In this scenario, while the man had initial involvement of both legs and arms, the final state of paralysis affecting mainly the lower trunk and legs does not fit the description of quadriplegia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Paraplegia: This condition involves paralysis of the lower half of the body, including both legs, but does not affect the arms. The description of initial involvement of the arms and later improvement to primarily affect the lower trunk and legs suggests a condition other than paraplegia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Monoplegia: This refers to paralysis affecting only one limb. Since the man's condition affected multiple areas of his body, monoplegia is not the correct diagnosis."}]},{"n":17,"q":"A lumbar puncture was performed on a patient in a sitting position, and the patient is now experiencing a very severe headache. Which of the following is NOT a recommended management strategy for a post-lumbar puncture headache?","o":[{"t":"Lie in a supine position"},{"t":"Administer intravenous caffeine"},{"t":"Restrict water intake"},{"t":"Perform a low-volume blood patch"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Restrict water intake:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Restrict water intake : Contrary to previous advice, increasing fluid intake (including water) is generally recommended to help replenish cerebrospinal fluid and support recovery. Restricting water intake is not advised."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Lie in a supine position: Encouraging the patient to lie flat can help reduce the headache by minimizing cerebrospinal fluid leakage and allowing the dura to seal itself."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Administer intravenous caffeine: Caffeine is known to help constrict blood vessels and may reduce the headache by improving cerebrospinal fluid production. Oral caffeine or beverages like tea and coffee can also be beneficial, but intravenous caffeine is often preferred in more severe cases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Perform a low-volume blood patch : If the headache persists despite conservative measures, a blood patch may be performed. This involves taking a small amount of the patient's blood and injecting it into the epidural space to seal the leak and alleviate the headache."}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with a unilateral throbbing headache that worsens with exposure to bright light. She occasionally skips breakfast. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Hunger headache"},{"t":"Migraine"},{"t":"Tension headache"},{"t":"Cluster headache"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Migraine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition is characterized by unilateral, throbbing headaches that may be accompanied by nausea, sensitivity to light (photophobia), and sometimes visual disturbances such as zig-zag lines or blurred vision (aura). The headache often worsens with bright light and can last from 4 to 72 hours, with an average duration of around 24 hours. Skipping meals can also trigger migraines."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Hunger headache: Typically bilateral rather than unilateral and generally does not present with photophobia or throbbing pain. It is often associated with low blood sugar levels due to missed meals."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tension headache: Usually bilateral, with a steady, pressing quality rather than throbbing. It is not typically aggravated by light and does not include nausea or significant sensitivity to sound or light."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Cluster headache : This type is characterized by severe, unilateral pain around the eye, often accompanied by autonomic symptoms like eye watering or nasal congestion. It does not generally present with sensitivity to light and the headache episodes are typically shorter but occur frequently in clusters."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 62-year-old patient presents with left-sided arm and leg weakness, right-sided facial paralysis, and difficulty with horizontal eye movements. Based on the clinical presentation, which of the following syndromes is most consistent with these symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Foville syndrome"},{"t":"Benedict's syndrome"},{"t":"Millard-Gubler syndrome"},{"t":"Wallenberg syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Millard-Gubler syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Millard-Gubler syndrome, also known as ventral pontine syndrome, is characterized by a combination of ipsilateral cranial nerve (CN) VI and VII palsy with contralateral hemiplegia. The clinical presentation in this case—left-sided arm and leg weakness (contralateral hemiplegia), right-sided facial paralysis (ipsilateral CN VII palsy), and difficulty with horizontal eye movements (ipsilateral CN VI palsy)—is consistent with Millard-Gubler syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This syndrome occurs due to a lesion in the ventral pons, affecting the corticospinal tract (which causes contralateral hemiplegia) and the fascicles of the abducens (CN VI) and facial (CN VII) nerves, leading to ipsilateral facial paralysis and difficulty in horizontal eye movements."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Foville syndrome: This syndrome also involves a lesion in the pons, but it typically affects the paramedian region, leading to ipsilateral facial paralysis, horizontal gaze palsy, and contralateral hemiplegia. However, Foville syndrome often includes additional symptoms like loss of conjugate gaze due to involvement of the pontine tegmentum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Benedict's syndrome: This syndrome is caused by a lesion in the midbrain, involving the red nucleus and the third nerve fascicles. It leads to ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy (ptosis, dilated pupil, and loss of most eye movements) and contralateral ataxia and tremor. It does not match the symptomatology of the case described."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Wallenberg syndrome: Also known as lateral medullary syndrome, this condition is due to a lesion in the lateral part of the medulla oblongata. It results in ipsilateral facial pain and temperature sensation loss, contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation on the body, dysphagia, hoarseness, and vertigo. It does not involve cranial nerves VI and VII or cause contralateral hemiplegia."}]},{"n":20,"q":"Patient is having a headache and confusion. A brain tumor diagnosis is made. Family history reveals brain and kidney tumors. Choose the correct option:","o":[{"t":"Neurofibromatosis"},{"t":"Li fraumeni syndrome"},{"t":"VHL Syndrome"},{"t":"Churg strauss syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - VHL syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome is a genetic condition characterized by the development of various tumors, including brain tumors (often hemangioblastomas), kidney tumors (renal cell carcinoma), and other tumors like pheochromocytomas. The family history of both brain and kidney tumors aligns with VHL syndrome. This condition is due to mutations in the VHL gene."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome is a genetic condition characterized by the development of various tumors, including brain tumors (often hemangioblastomas), kidney tumors (renal cell carcinoma), and other tumors like pheochromocytomas. The family history of both brain and kidney tumors aligns with VHL syndrome. This condition is due to mutations in the VHL gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Neurofibromatosis: Neurofibromatosis (NF) has two main types: NF1 and NF2. NF1 is associated with cutaneous neurofibromas, café-au-lait spots, and optic nerve tumors, while NF2 is associated with bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) and other central nervous system tumors. Although brain tumors are present, NF does not typically present with kidney tumors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Li-Fraumeni syndrome: Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary condition associated with a higher risk of several cancers, including breast cancer, sarcomas, brain tumors, and adrenal cortex tumors. While it is associated with brain tumors, it is not typically associated with kidney tumors. The syndrome is caused by mutations in the TP53 gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Churg-Strauss syndrome: Churg-Strauss syndrome, now known as eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA), is an autoimmune condition that affects small and medium-sized blood vessels. It primarily involves respiratory symptoms, asthma, and vasculitis, but it is not associated with brain and kidney tumors."}]},{"n":21,"q":"What is the confirmatory test for the above condition?","o":[{"t":"CBNAAT of CSF"},{"t":"PET Scan"},{"t":"NMR Spectroscopy"},{"t":"MRI aerial scan"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - NMR Spectroscopy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When differentiating between tuberculoma and neurocysticercosis in cases of ring-enhancing lesions, NMR Spectroscopy (Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy) provides detailed metabolic and chemical information about the brain lesions. This technique can help differentiate between various types of lesions based on their biochemical signatures, making it valuable in distinguishing between tuberculomas (due to Mycobacterium tuberculosis) and neurocysticercosis (due to Taenia solium)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NMR Spectroscopy analyzes the metabolic byproducts and chemical composition of the lesions, which can help in differentiating between conditions like tuberculoma and neurocysticercosis. It can provide information about the presence of specific metabolites and biochemical changes associated with each condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - CBNAAT of CSF:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While CBNAAT is excellent for detecting Mycobacterium tuberculosis DNA and is very useful in diagnosing CNS tuberculosis, it is not typically used to distinguish between tuberculoma and neurocysticercosis directly. It confirms the presence of tuberculosis but does not provide biochemical information about the lesion itself."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - PET Scan:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PET scans show metabolic activity in tissues and can be helpful in assessing lesions, but they do not provide detailed biochemical or chemical information specific to tuberculoma vs. neurocysticercosis. They are more used for overall lesion characterization rather than definitive diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - MRI aerial scan:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This term is not a standard diagnostic modality in clinical practice. MRI is used to visualize lesions and their characteristics but does not provide specific confirmation of tuberculoma or neurocysticercosis. It helps in lesion assessment but is not confirmatory for distinguishing between these two conditions."}],"qi":[8484]},{"n":22,"q":"The CSF findings in bacterial meningitis would include which of the following?","o":[{"t":"Elevated protein and decreased glucose"},{"t":"Normal white blood cell count"},{"t":"Decreased protein and elevated glucose"},{"t":"Elevated WBC count with normal glucose"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In bacterial meningitis , there is characteristic elevation of CSF protein due to blood-brain barrier disruption and decreased CSF glucose due to increased glucose utilization by bacteria and white blood cells. The typical CSF glucose level in bacterial meningitis is less than 40% of serum glucose. The protein elevation is typically marked, often greater than 100 mg/dL, reflecting significant inflammation and blood-brain barrier dysfunction ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CSF Findings in various types of meningitis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type Opening Pressur Predominant Protein Gluco Gross Appearan"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"e Cell Type se ce"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bacterial Elevated Polymorphonucl Elevated Decre Turbid Meningitis ear Neutrophils ased"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(PMNs)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Fungal/Tubercul Elevated Lymphocytes Elevated Decre Straw-colored ar Meningitis ased (TB meningitis)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Viral Meningitis Normal or Slightl Lymphocytes Normal or Slightl Norm Normal or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"y Elevated y Elevated al Turbid"}]},{"n":23,"q":"A 35-year-old woman presents with fluctuating muscle weakness, especially affecting her speech and swallowing. Repetitive nerve stimulation shows a decremental response. Her blood tests are negative for acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies. Which of the following antibodies is most likely responsible for her symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies"},{"t":"Anti-MuSK antibodies"},{"t":"Anti-TPO antibodies"},{"t":"Anti-Ro antibodies"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Anti-MuSK antibodies"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MuSK is essential for clustering AChRs at the neuromuscular junction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-MuSK antibodies are found in AChR-negative patients, often presenting with bulbar muscle weakness (affecting speech and swallowing)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Autoantibodies in Myasthenia Gravis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder characterized by muscle weakness due to disrupted neuromuscular transmission."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Types of Autoantibodies"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Autoa Target Effects Associated Features ntibod y"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"AChR Acetylcholine receptors Blocks signal Most common antib (AChR) on the postsynapti transmission, leading odies c membrane to muscle weakness"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MuSK Muscle-specific kinase Impairs AChR Found in AChR-negative antib (MuSK) clustering and patients; associated with odies maintenance bulbar muscle weakness"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(speech, swallowing, chewing issues)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic Tests Repetitive Nerve Stimulation : Shows a decremental response (decline in muscle activity with repeated stimulation), indicating faulty neuromuscular transmission. Blood Tests : Detect the presence of AChR and MuSK antibodies ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Repetitive Nerve Stimulation : Shows a decremental response (decline in muscle activity with repeated stimulation), indicating faulty neuromuscular transmission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood Tests : Detect the presence of AChR and MuSK antibodies ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Repetitive Nerve Stimulation : Shows a decremental response (decline in muscle activity with repeated stimulation), indicating faulty neuromuscular transmission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood Tests : Detect the presence of AChR and MuSK antibodies ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies (Option A) : Seen in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) , not associated with neuromuscular transmission issues."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-TPO antibodies (Option C) : Linked with autoimmune thyroid disorders (e.g., Hashimoto’s thyroiditis)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anti-Ro antibodies (Option D) : Found in Sjogren’s syndrome and neonatal lupus , not relevant to myasthenia gravis."}]},{"n":24,"q":"What is the probable cause of the patient's condition based on the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) specimen shown below, which exhibits mononuclear cytosis, increased proteins, and decreased sugars?","o":[{"t":"Tuberculous meningitis"},{"t":"Aseptic meningitis"},{"t":"Bacterial meningitis"},{"t":"Chemical meningitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A -Tuberculous meningitis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CSF findings in tubercular meningitis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mononuclear cytosis (increased number of mononuclear cells, which are ordinarily lymphocytes or monocytes)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated proteins (suggestive of inflammation)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low sugars (suggestive of increased metabolism by the inflammatory cells)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B- Aseptic meningitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aseptic meningitis ordinarily incorporates a shorter length of indications than TB meningitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C- Bacterial meningitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is an acute disease caused by microbes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Findings: Elevated opening pressure, increased PMN"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased Protein and turbid in appearance."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D- Chemical meningitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is caused by the disturbance of the meninges by different chemicals or medicines."}],"qi":[8491]},{"n":25,"q":"A female patient presents to you with a unilateral headache. It is associated with nausea, photophobia, and phonophobia. What is the drug of choice for acute management?","o":[{"t":"Flunarizine"},{"t":"Sumatriptan"},{"t":"Propranolol"},{"t":"Topiramate"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Sumatriptan:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's introduction suggests a migraine headache."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sumatriptan is a serotonin 5-HT1B/1D receptor agonist and is considered the drug of choice for acute administration of migraines."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These can be administered via oral, subcutaneous or nasal route."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triptans are contrainidcated in pregnancy, prinzmetal angina, peripheral arterial disease, Basilar migraine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Flunarizine : It is a calcium channel blocker that's used for migraine prophylaxis and not for acute administration. It can take weeks to months to attain a therapeutic effect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Propranolol : It is a beta-blocker that's moreover utilized for migraine prophylaxis and not for acute administration. It can take weeks to months to realize a therapeutic effect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Topiramate : It is an anticonvulsant that's moreover utilized for migraine prophylaxis and not for acute administration. It can take weeks to months to realize a therapeutic effect."}]},{"n":26,"q":"In hypoglossal nerve injury, which of the following is seen?","o":[{"t":"Tongue deviation to the contralateral side"},{"t":"Tongue deviation to the same side"},{"t":"Falling of the tongue"},{"t":"Inability in tongue protrusion"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Tongue deviation to the same side:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The hypoglossal nerve is the 12th cranial nerve that controls the movement of the tongue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the hypoglossal nerve is injured, tongue deviation to the same side as the injury is the most common symptom. This is because the muscles on the affected side of the tongue become weak or paralyzed while the muscles on the unaffected side remain functional."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Tongue deviation to the contralateral side: Tongue deviation to the contralateral side is incorrect as it suggests that the tongue would move towards the opposite side of the injury. However, this is not a typical presentation of hypoglossal nerve injury."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Falling of the tongue: Falling of the tongue is also incorrect, as this is a nonspecific symptom that could occur due to various reasons, including weakness or paralysis of the tongue muscles."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Inability in tongue protrusion: Inability in tongue protrusion is also incorrect. This symptom would suggest complete paralysis of the tongue muscles on the affected side, which is not always the case with hypoglossal nerve injury."}]},{"n":27,"q":"What is the diagnosis for a 24-year-old female patient who presents with fever, headache, ataxia, nausea, and vomiting for the past 3 days? The patient exhibits neck rigidity and hip and knee flexion upon passive flexion of the neck. A lumbar puncture was performed, and the CSF findings are provided below. Opening pressure – increased Color – cloudy white WBCs – 7000/µl; neutrophil predominant Sugar – 15 mg/dl CSF/serum glucose ratio – 0.2 Protein – 70 mg/dl Latex agglutination – positive Limulus lysate test – negative","o":[{"t":"Bacterial meningitis"},{"t":"Tubercular meningitis"},{"t":"Viral meningitis"},{"t":"Fungal meningitis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of the patient, including fever, headache, ataxia, nausea, vomiting, and neck rigidity, suggests meningitis. The findings from the CSF analysis also support this diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of increased opening pressure, cloudy white color, neutrophil-predominant leukocytosis, low CSF/serum glucose ratio, and elevated protein levels are all indicative of meningitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The positive latex agglutination test and negative Limulus lysate test suggest bacterial etiology."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Tubercular meningitis. The positive latex agglutination test is suggestive of tuberculosis, and the presence of neutrophil-predominant leukocytosis is typical of early-stage tubercular meningitis. The low CSF/serum glucose ratio is also a characteristic finding in tubercular meningitis. But it is not the correct option."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Viral meningitis: In viral meningitis, the CSF typically shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, with normal or slightly elevated protein levels and normal glucose levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Fungal meningitis can also present with similar symptoms and CSF findings, including increased opening pressure, low glucose, and elevated protein. However, fungal meningitis is rare and usually occurs in immunocompromised individuals."}]},{"n":28,"q":"Which of the following type of hypersensitivity reactions is seen in myasthenia gravis?","o":[{"t":"Type 1 hypersensitivity reaction"},{"t":"Type 2 hypersensitivity reaction"},{"t":"Type 3 hypersensitivity reaction"},{"t":"Type 4 hypersensitivity reaction"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Type 2 hypersensitivity reaction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The type of hypersensitivity reaction seen in myasthenia gravis is a type 2 hypersensitivity reaction. Hypersensitivity reactions are exaggerated immune responses that can cause tissue damage and disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease, most commonly (80% of cases) caused by antibodies to acetylcholine receptors in the post-junctional membrane of the neuromuscular junction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The autoantibodies in myasthenia gravis are primarily of the IgG subtype and lead to complement-mediated destruction of the acetylcholine receptors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Type 1 hypersensitivity reaction: These reactions typically occur within minutes of exposure and can cause symptoms such as hives, itching, and anaphylaxis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Type 3 hypersensitivity reaction: Examples of type 3 hypersensitivity reactions include systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and rheumatoid arthritis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Type 4 hypersensitivity reaction: Examples of type 4 hypersensitivity reactions include contact dermatitis and some forms of drug reactions."}]},{"n":29,"q":"A young patient presents with a severe headache. He has never experienced a headache of this severity before. He has hypertension and nuchal rigidity on examination. Which of the following could be the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Cluster headache"},{"t":"Subarachnoid haemorrhage"},{"t":"Encephalitis"},{"t":"Meningitis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Subarachnoid haemorrhage:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A subarachnoid hemorrhage is a type of bleeding that occurs in the space between the brain and the thin tissues that cover it. It is often caused by the rupture of a blood vessel, such as an aneurysm ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A severe headache is the hallmark symptom of a subarachnoid hemorrhage, often described as the \"worst headache of my life.\" The presence of hypertension and nuchal rigidity further supports this diagnosis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other features depending on the location of the bleed includes abulia, aphasia, 6th cranial nerve palsy."},{"k":"img","v":8605},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Non-contrast CT head shows “Star of Death”, due to the accumulation of blood in basal cisterns. The condition has 35% mortality Rate if untreated."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Cluster headache : Cluster headaches are a type of primary headache disorder characterised by severe, unilateral (one-sided) pain that is often centred around the eye ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Encephalitis : Encephalitis often presents with other symptoms such as fever, altered mental status, seizures, and focal neurological deficits ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Meningitis : Meningitis commonly presents with symptoms such as severe headache, fever, neck stiffness (nuchal rigidity), and sensitivity to light ( photophobia )."}],"qi":[8497]},{"n":30,"q":"What is the likely diagnosis for a 26-year-old male who had a respiratory tract infection 4 weeks ago and has been experiencing progressive, symmetrical weakness, starting in the lower limbs and gradually affecting the upper limbs? The weakness has made it difficult for him to stand or walk for the past 2 weeks. On examination, there is a lack of reflexes, but pain sensation and proprioception are normal.","o":[{"t":"Guillain Barre syndrome"},{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Polymyositis"},{"t":"Multiple sclerosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Guillain-Barre syndrome is an autoimmune polyradiculopathy causing Patchy segmental demyelination of spinal cord and Peripheral nerves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The weakness in GBS is symmetrical and ascending , meaning it starts in the lower limbs and moves upward symmetrically."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features include"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness in legs causing rubbery legs and instability to stand"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progression of weakness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Truncal paralysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Quadriplegia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neck floppiness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bulbar palsy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proprioception loss"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Myasthenia gravis: Weakness in myasthenia gravis typically worsens with repeated use of the affected muscles and improves with rest. In addition, myasthenia gravis often affects the muscles of the face and eyes before involving the limb"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Polymyositis: Polymyositis is an inflammatory muscle disease that causes symmetric weakness in the proximal muscles (muscle)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Multiple sclerosis: Multiple sclerosis typically does not present with ascending weakness or areflexia as seen in GBS."}]},{"n":31,"q":"What is the recommended treatment for a patient who is HIV-positive and presents with a high-grade fever, positive Kernig's sign, and laboratory findings of reduced glucose, increased protein, and increased leukocytes in the CSF, leading to a diagnosis of cryptococcal meningitis?","o":[{"t":"Vancomycin"},{"t":"High dose fluconazole with flucytosine"},{"t":"Voriconazole"},{"t":"Liposomal amphotericin B"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Liposomal amphotericin B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Liposomal amphotericin B is the preferred initial therapy for severe cryptococcal meningitis due to its higher efficacy and better penetration into the central nervous system ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment of Cryptococcal meningitis LAMB (Liposomal amphotericin B) and 5-Fluorocytosine for 2 weeks. Fluconazole is given subsequently for 6 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"LAMB (Liposomal amphotericin B) and 5-Fluorocytosine for 2 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluconazole is given subsequently for 6 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"LAMB (Liposomal amphotericin B) and 5-Fluorocytosine for 2 weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluconazole is given subsequently for 6 weeks."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Vancomycin: Vancomycin is primarily used to treat bacterial infections, particularly those caused by MRSA ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - High dose fluconazole with flucytosine: High dose fluconazole with flucytosine: Fluconazole is commonly used for the treatment and maintenance therapy of cryptococcal meningitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Voriconazole: Voriconazole is primarily used to treat invasive aspergillosis and other filamentous fungal infections."}]},{"n":32,"q":"What could be the likely cause of the headaches experienced by a woman who has a history of headaches for the past 9 months that worsen when lying down and improve throughout the day? Additionally, she reports using oral contraceptives and her fundal examination reveals papilledema, with no presence of focal neurological deficits.","o":[{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Temporal arteritis"},{"t":"Chronic migraine"},{"t":"Pseudotumor cerebri"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Pseudotumor cerebri:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pseudotumor cerebri, also known as idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH), is the probable cause of the woman's headaches based on the information provided."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pseudotumor cerebri is a condition characterized by increased intracranial pressure without any identifiable cause , such as a tumor."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features: Headaches Papilledema Vision changes Pulsatile tinnitus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headaches"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Papilledema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vision changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulsatile tinnitus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Headaches"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Papilledema"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vision changes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulsatile tinnitus"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Myasthenia gravis: Results in muscle weakness and fatigue. It is not typically associated with headaches or papilledema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Temporal arteritis: Also known as giant cell arteritis, is an inflammatory condition that affects the blood vessels, especially the temporal arteries."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Chronic migraine: It is a subtype of migraine headache that occurs on 15 or more days per month for at least three months."}]},{"n":33,"q":"What is the probable medical condition of a young male patient who experiences drooping of the upper eyelid and muscular weakness that appears to intensify during the evening and alleviate in the morning, and is relieved by neostigmine?","o":[{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Huntington chorea"},{"t":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"},{"t":"External ophthalmoplegia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Myasthenia gravis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder characterised by voluntary muscle weakness and fatigue caused by antibodies that attack the neuromuscular junction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ptosis is a common symptom, as is muscle weakness, which tends to worsen with activity and improve with rest."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other symptoms include weakness, easy fatigability, sustained upward gaze, snarling facies, difficulty in chewing and swallowing, tongue weakness, bulbar weakness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neostigmine increases the amount of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction and can help alleviate Myasthenia gravis symptoms."},{"k":"img","v":8610},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Huntington chorea: Huntington's chorea is a genetic condition that causes involuntary movements, cognitive decline, and psychiatric symptoms ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: In Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), while weakness is a common symptom of ALS, the pattern of worsening in the evening and improving in the morning, with relief from neostigmine, is unusual."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - External ophthalmoplegia: External ophthalmoplegia does not usually present with diurnal variation or respond to neostigmine in the same way that the case described did."}]},{"n":34,"q":"Bell's palsy involves:","o":[{"t":"Ipsilateral upper and lower halves of the face"},{"t":"Contralateral upper and lower halves of the face"},{"t":"Ipsilateral lower half of the face"},{"t":"Contralateral lower half of the face"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Ipsilateral upper and lower halves of the face:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bell's palsy is the most common form of fascial paralysis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bell's palsy causes paralysis or weakness in both the upper and lower halves of the face on the same side."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of incomplete paralysis in the first week is the most favorable prognostic sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Taste sensation may be lost unilaterally, and hyperacusis may be pres- ent."},{"k":"img","v":8613},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option B, C & D: Bell's palsy, on the other hand, affects the facial nerve on the same side of the body, so the symptoms would not be contralateral."}]},{"n":35,"q":"The patient's wife brings him to the outpatient department (OPD) and reports his difficulty in expressing emotions and lack of involvement in daily activities. During the examination, the presence of resting tremors and rigidity is observed. Considering the potential diagnosis, which specific region of the patient's brain is impacted?","o":[{"t":"Basal ganglia"},{"t":"Hippocampus"},{"t":"Cerebellum"},{"t":"Premotor cortex"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Basal ganglia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"According to the vignette, the patient suffers from Parkinson’s disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parkinson’s disease is a neurological disorder that occurs due to damage to neural cells in the substantia nigra."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Substantia nigra is a part of the basal ganglia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Deficiency of dopamine fl"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased discharge from output region of basal ganglia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Excessive inhibition of the thalamus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Paucity of movements (bradykinesia/akinesia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parkinson’s disease results in tremors, rigidity, bradykinesia or akinesia and postural instability, similar to the patient’s."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hippocampus: The hippocampus is responsible for learning and memory. Damage to it causes amnesia that prevents patients from forming new memories and/or recalling old ones."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cerebellum: The cerebellum is responsible for the coordination of motor functions. A defect in it causes ataxia, Intention tremors, akinesia, bradykinesia, dysphagia and fatigue of \\which are absent in the patient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Premotor cortex: The premotor cortex is responsible for the selection of movements. It also helps initiate movements. Damage to the premotor cortex causes apraxia- the inability to perform skilled actions- and deficits in contralateral fine motor control."}]},{"n":36,"q":"What is the most probable cause for the symptoms of seizures, along with a history of fever, headache, and confusion, in a male patient who arrived at the emergency room? An MRI brain scan revealed inflammation in the bitemporal lobe.","o":[{"t":"Cytomegalovirus"},{"t":"Toxoplasma gondii"},{"t":"Herpes simplex virus"},{"t":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Herpes simplex virus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"According to the vignette, the patient has a headache, fever and confusion, with a presentation of seizures in the emergency room. These signs are very indicative of meningitis, for which an MRI done of the patient shows inflammation of the temporal lobe."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temporal lobe involvement during meningitis is a pathognomic feature of Herpes Meningoencephalitis ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Imaging by CT scan may show low-density lesions in the temporal lobes but MRI is more sensitive in detecting early abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other investigations: Bloody CSF Periodic lateralized epileptiform discharge (PLED) in EEG Investigation of choice- PCR HASV-1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bloody CSF"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Periodic lateralized epileptiform discharge (PLED) in EEG"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Investigation of choice- PCR HASV-1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bloody CSF"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Periodic lateralized epileptiform discharge (PLED) in EEG"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Investigation of choice- PCR HASV-1"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Cytomegalovirus: Cytomegalovirus preferentially infects the ventricular and/or subventricular areas as it replicates in the neural stem or precursor cells residing in that area."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Toxoplasma gondii: Toxoplasma gondii predominantly affects the brain's white matter surrounding the basal and basal ganglia. MRI of the brain in toxoplasmosis shows multiple ring-enhancing regions all over the brain’s cerebral hemispheres."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Mycobacterium tuberculosis: Mycobacterium tuberculosis causes inflammation of the meninges alone and very rarely causes encephalitis. On an MRI, there is an enhancement of the leptomeningeal junction because of the sedimentation of infectious material there."}]},{"n":37,"q":"A 65-year-old patient presents with tremors, rigidity, and bradykinesia. Pathological examination shows the presence of Lewy bodies within the neurons. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Parkinson's disease"},{"t":"Prion disease"},{"t":"Huntington's chorea"},{"t":"Alzheimer's disease"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Parkinson’s disease :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pathological hallmarks of PD are depletion of the pigmented dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra and the presence of a-synuclein and other protein inclusions in nigral cells (Lewy bodies)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lewy bodies are also found in the basal ganglia, brainstem and cortex, and increase with disease progression"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Prion disease: Prion diseases are rare, fatal neurodegenerative disorders caused by misfolded prion proteins (PrP) in the brain."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Huntington’s chorea: Huntington’s disease is a progressive brain disorder that causes uncontrolled movements, emotional problems, and loss of thinking ability (cognition)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Alzheimer's disease: In Alzheimer’s disease, on biopsy, there is an increase in beta-amyloid content or tau proteins."}]},{"n":38,"q":"A patient came to the medical OPD with complaints of polyuria. He has a history of undergoing total hypophysectomy. His Na+ levels were found to be 155 mEq/ L, urine osmolarity was 200 mOsm/L. What is the definitive management in this patient?","o":[{"t":"DDAVP for 2 weeks and then discontinue"},{"t":"DDAVP supplementation for lifelong"},{"t":"Upsetting of receptors so no treatment is required"},{"t":"Thiazides for 2 weeks"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - DDAVP supplementation for lifelong:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above findings following hypophysectomy are indicative of central diabetes insipidus which is a result of inefficient function of the pituitary."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DDAVP (des-amino-des- aspartate-arginine vasopressin) is a synthetic analog of vasopressin, having a prolonged action profile with minimal vasopressor activity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can be administered orally, intranasally, subcutaneously, or intravenously."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This patient requires its administration lifelong due to the loss of pituitary function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The dose of DDAVP should be adjusted on the basis of serum sodium concentrations and/or osmolality."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - DDAVP for 2 weeks and then discontinue: Since the patient has central diabetes insipidus due to the loss of pituitary function permanently, the patient requires DDAVP (vasopressin) for a lifetime and not 2 weeks only."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Upsetting of receptors so no treatment is required: Since the patient has a deranged serum sodium level and an abnormal urine osmolality, treatment needs to be provided."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Thiazides for 2 weeks: Thiazides are used to treat nephrogenic diabetes insipidus which results due to the absence of vasopressin receptors on the nephrons."}]},{"n":39,"q":"A 50-year-old male patient presented with left-sided hemiparesis. Damage to which part of the internal capsule leads to this presentation?","o":[{"t":"Retrolentiform"},{"t":"Sublentiform"},{"t":"Anterior limb"},{"t":"Posterior limb"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Posterior limb:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The posterior limb of the internal capsule contains the corticospinal tract, which is responsible for motor control of the limbs. Damage to this area can lead to contralateral hemiparesis, as seen in the patient's left-sided weakness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Retrolentiform: Damage to the retrolentiform part of the internal capsule would typically result in sensory deficits, such as loss of sensation or abnormal sensation, rather than hemiparesis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Sublentiform: Similar to the retrolentiform part, damage to the sublentiform part of the internal capsule is more likely to produce sensory deficits rather than hemiparesis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Anterior limb: Damage to the anterior limb of the internal capsule can cause a variety of symptoms, including contralateral weakness, but it usually affects the face more than the limbs."}]},{"n":40,"q":"Where is the site of the lesion that leads to the occurrence of hemiballismus in a patient displaying forceful, flinging movements?","o":[{"t":"Putamen"},{"t":"Subthalamic nucleus"},{"t":"Caudate nucleus"},{"t":"Globus pallidus"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Subthalamic nucleus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ballism is a violent form of choreiform movement composed of wild, flinging, large-amplitude movements most frequently affecting proximal limb muscles on one side of the body (hemiballism)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is typically caused by a lesion in the subthalamic nucleus (STN) of the basal ganglia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Putamen: Lesions in the putamen are associated with chorea, not hemiballismus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Caudate nucleus: Lesions in the caudate nucleus are associated with Huntington's disease, which causes chorea as well."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Globus pallidus: Lesions in the globus pallidus can cause various movement disorders, such as dystonia or Parkinson's disease, but they are not specifically associated with hemiballismus."}]},{"n":41,"q":"What is the cause of the following symptoms in a patient: Horner's syndrome and loss of pain and temperature sensations in the face on the same side, along with vertigo, numbness, loss of sweating, and dysarthria on the opposite side? A. Medial medulla B. Lateral medulla C. Ventromedial medulla D. Lateral medulla including nucleus ambiguous and spinothalamic tract","o":[{"t":"Only B"},{"t":"B, C, D"},{"t":"A, B, C"},{"t":"A and B"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Only B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The symptoms described in the question are consistent with a syndrome known as Wallenberg syndrome or lateral medullary syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wallenberg syndrome is caused by a lesion in the lateral part of the medulla oblongata, specifically involving the lateral medulla."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The features of Wallenberg syndrome - Ipsilateral Horner's syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis), Ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature sensations in the face (due to involvement of the spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract), Vertigo with numbness (due to involvement of the vestibular nuclei) Dysarthria on the contralateral side (due to involvement of the corticobulbar fibers)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral Horner's syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis),"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature sensations in the face (due to involvement of the spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract),"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vertigo with numbness (due to involvement of the vestibular nuclei)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dysarthria on the contralateral side (due to involvement of the corticobulbar fibers)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral Horner's syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis),"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature sensations in the face (due to involvement of the spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract),"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vertigo with numbness (due to involvement of the vestibular nuclei)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dysarthria on the contralateral side (due to involvement of the corticobulbar fibers)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer."}]},{"n":42,"q":"What could be the possible diagnosis for a female patient who has experienced a loss of pain and temperature sensation, but still retains her touch sensation, as indicated by imaging findings that reveal cavitation around the central canal?","o":[{"t":"Brown-Sequard syndrome"},{"t":"Tabes dorsalis"},{"t":"Syringobulbia"},{"t":"Syringomyelia"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Syringomyelia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syringomyelia is asymetrial dilation of the central canal of the spinal cord causing pressure on the adjoint tracts"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dissociated sensory loss is indicative of syringomyelia- Loss of pain and temperature, but touch, proprioception and vibration are sparred."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other clinical features include Cape distribution of sensory loss Areflexic weakness in hand Weakness in shoulder and lower neck muscles Spasticity in the leg: Ipsilateral or bilateral Bladder and bowel involvement- if sacral fibers are affected Facial numbness-The C2 segment of the spinal cord is affected Horner syndrome: Involvement of sympathetic fibers Cough-induced headache: In Arnold chiari type-1 Syringobulbia: Palatal, vocal cord paralysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cape distribution of sensory loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Areflexic weakness in hand"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness in shoulder and lower neck muscles"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spasticity in the leg: Ipsilateral or bilateral"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bladder and bowel involvement- if sacral fibers are affected"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Facial numbness-The C2 segment of the spinal cord is affected"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Horner syndrome: Involvement of sympathetic fibers"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cough-induced headache: In Arnold chiari type-1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syringobulbia: Palatal, vocal cord paralysis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cape distribution of sensory loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Areflexic weakness in hand"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness in shoulder and lower neck muscles"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spasticity in the leg: Ipsilateral or bilateral"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bladder and bowel involvement- if sacral fibers are affected"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Facial numbness-The C2 segment of the spinal cord is affected"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Horner syndrome: Involvement of sympathetic fibers"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cough-induced headache: In Arnold chiari type-1"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syringobulbia: Palatal, vocal cord paralysis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Brown-Sequard syndrome: Brown-Sequard syndrome involves loss of joint position and vibration senses and motor deficits on the same side of the lesion and contralateral pain and temperature is lost."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Tabes dorsalis: Tabes dorsalis is associated with sensory ataxia, lightning-like pain, and impaired proprioception, differing from the symptoms described."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Syringobulbia: Syringobulbia is a clinical feature of Syringomyelia presenting as palatal and vocal cord paralysis"}]},{"n":43,"q":"All of the following aid in the localization of the lesion to the spinal cord except?","o":[{"t":"Contralateral hemiplegia"},{"t":"Fasciculation at the level of lesion"},{"t":"Upper motor neuron lesion and lower motor neuron lesion"},{"t":"Bladder involvement"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Contralateral hemiplegia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral hemiplegia refers to weakness or paralysis affecting one side of the body that is opposite to the side of the lesion. However, contralateral hemiplegia is not characteristic of lesions within the spinal cord. It is more commonly associated with brainstem lesions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Fasciculation at the level of the lesion: Fasciculation is observed at the uppermost level of the lesion in the spinal cord, it can provide clues about the involvement of the corresponding spinal cord segment."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion and lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion: UMN lesions involve the descending motor pathways (corticospinal) originating from the brain and traveling through the spinal cord, while LMN lesions affect the peripheral nerves or nerve roots exiting the spinal cord."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Bladder involvement: Bladder involvement, such as urinary retention or incontinence, can indicate dysfunction or damage to the nerves controlling bladder function within the spinal cord."}]},{"n":44,"q":"Which of the following is not characteristic of Brown Sequard syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Complete transection of spinal cord"},{"t":"Ipsilateral loss of vibration & touch"},{"t":"Contralateral loss of pain & temperature"},{"t":"Ipsilateral loss of proprioception"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Complete transection of spinal cord:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complete transection of the spinal cord is not a feature of Brown-Séquard syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brown-Séquard syndrome is a specific pattern of spinal cord injury characterized by a hemisection of the spinal cord (one half of the spinal cord is damaged or severed)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presentation Ipsilateral Proprioception loss Spastic weakness due to corticospinal tract involvement Development of radicular pain at the level of lesion At level of lesion muscles- fasciculation, areflexia Contralateral Pain and temperature loss- 2 segments below"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral Proprioception loss Spastic weakness due to corticospinal tract involvement Development of radicular pain at the level of lesion At level of lesion muscles- fasciculation, areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proprioception loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spastic weakness due to corticospinal tract involvement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Development of radicular pain at the level of lesion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"At level of lesion muscles- fasciculation, areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral Pain and temperature loss- 2 segments below"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain and temperature loss- 2 segments below"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral Proprioception loss Spastic weakness due to corticospinal tract involvement Development of radicular pain at the level of lesion At level of lesion muscles- fasciculation, areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proprioception loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spastic weakness due to corticospinal tract involvement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Development of radicular pain at the level of lesion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"At level of lesion muscles- fasciculation, areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral Pain and temperature loss- 2 segments below"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain and temperature loss- 2 segments below"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proprioception loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spastic weakness due to corticospinal tract involvement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Development of radicular pain at the level of lesion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"At level of lesion muscles- fasciculation, areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain and temperature loss- 2 segments below"},{"k":"img","v":8623},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ipsilateral loss of vibration and touch: This is a characteristic feature of Brown-Séquard syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Contralateral loss of pain and temperature: This is another feature of Brown-Séquard syndrome. The side of the body opposite to the lesion (contralateral side) will exhibit a loss of pain and temperature sensation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ipsilateral loss of proprioception: This is also a feature of Brown-Séquard syndrome. The side of the body with the lesion (ipsilateral side) will experience a loss of proprioception, which refers to the ability to sense the position and movement of one's own body."}]},{"n":45,"q":"The following clinical examination was performed. What lesion would cause an exaggerated reflex?","o":[{"t":"Polyneuropathy"},{"t":"Radiculopathy"},{"t":"Upper motor neuron"},{"t":"Lower motor neuron"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Upper motor neuron:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Upper motor neuron lesion: An exaggerated reflex, also known as hyperreflexia, is a clinical finding associated with dysfunction or pathology involving the upper motor neuron system."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The weakness of upper motor neuron lesions is conversely more pronounced in the opposing muscle groups."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of inhibition leads to brisk reflexes and enhanced reflex patterns of movement, such as flexion withdrawal to noxious stimuli and spasms of extension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The increased tone is more apparent during rapid stretching (‘spastic catch’) but may quickly give way with sustained tension (the ‘clasp-knife’ phenomenon)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Polyneuropathy: Polyneuropathy typically results in sensory and motor deficits rather than exaggerated reflexes. In polyneuropathy, reflexes may be diminished or absent due to the involvement of peripheral nerves."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Radiculopathy: Although radiculopathy can lead to abnormal reflexes, the reflexes are usually diminished or weakened rather than exaggerated."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Lower motor neuron: Lower motor neuron lesions typically result in diminished or absent reflexes rather than exaggerated reflexes."}],"qi":[8507]},{"n":46,"q":"A young man is brought to the hospital with high-grade fever and altered consciousness. On examination, he had neck rigidity and pain when bending the neck. A lumbar puncture was performed, which showed a WBC count of 145 cells/µL, majorly lymphocytes, elevated opening pressure with protein of 120 mg/dL, and glucose level of 35 mg/dL. How will you manage this patient?","o":[{"t":"Piperacillin + Tazobactam"},{"t":"Vancomycin + Ceftriaxone"},{"t":"Amphotericin B + Flucytosine"},{"t":"Ceftriaxone + Ampicillin"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Amphotericin B + Flucytosine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the clinical presentation and the results of the lumbar puncture, this patient is most likely suffering from fungal meningitis. The WBC count is what gives away the diagnosis, hence, Amphotericin B an anti-fungal will be used for the treatment."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amphotericin B is typically used in conjunction with flucytosine to combat some fungal illnesses because it increases the uptake of flucytosine by fungi."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flucanazole is given subsequently for 6 weeks."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, B and D are not used to treat patients with fungal meningitis."}]},{"n":47,"q":"An elderly patient was brought to the OPD with complains tremors, stoop posture and slow movements. His son also gives history of forgetting day to day activities. What is the possible cause of this condition?","o":[{"t":"NPH"},{"t":"Hypothyroidism"},{"t":"Lewy body"},{"t":"Vitamin B12 deficiency"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Lewy body:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lewy body dementia neurodegenerative disorder is clinically characterised by dementia and signs of Parkinson’s disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is generally considered a progressive condition with irreversible cognitive decline."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other causes of irreversible dementia: Alzheimer's disease. Vascular dementia. Huntington's disease. Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease. Chronic traumatic encephalopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alzheimer's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vascular dementia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Huntington's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic traumatic encephalopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alzheimer's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vascular dementia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Huntington's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic traumatic encephalopathy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - NPH (Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus): NPH can cause a triad of symptoms known as Hakim's triad, which includes gait disturbances, urinary incontinence, and cognitive impairment."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hypothyroidism: Hypothyroidism can lead to various symptoms, including cognitive impairment, depression, and memory problems."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Vitamin B12 deficiency: Vitamin B12 deficiency can lead to neurological symptoms, including cognitive impairment and dementia-like symptoms."}]},{"n":48,"q":"What is observed in the case of an abnormal accumulation of misfolded protein?","o":[{"t":"Creutzfeldt jakob disease"},{"t":"Nephritic syndrome"},{"t":"Sickle cell anemia"},{"t":"Megaloblastic anemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A: Creutzfeldt jakob disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) is a neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the abnormal accumulation of misfolded prion proteins in the brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prions are infectious agents composed of abnormal isoforms of the prion protein (PrP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These misfolded proteins can induce the conversion of normal PrP into the abnormal form, leading to the accumulation of amyloid plaques in the brain tissue."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Nephritic syndrome: Nephritic syndrome is primarily associated with immune-mediated damage and not the accumulation of misfolded proteins."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Sickle cell anemia: In Sickle cell anemia, while there is an abnormality in the structure of hemoglobin in sickle cell anemia, it does not involve the accumulation of misfolded proteins as seen in CJD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Megaloblastic anemia: Megaloblastic anemia is a type of anemia characterized by the presence of large, immature red blood cells (megaloblasts) in the bone marrow. Megaloblastic anemia does not involve the accumulation of misfolded proteins."}]},{"n":49,"q":"What is the probable medical condition in a 37-year-old female patient who has been experiencing headaches for the past 6 months and has been using painkillers consistently? The severity of the headache recently worsened for a period of 3 days but improved upon discontinuing the analgesic medication.","o":[{"t":"Medication overuse headache"},{"t":"Tension headache"},{"t":"Chronic migraine"},{"t":"Cluster headache"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Medication overuse headache:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The use of analgesics and anti-migraine medications frequently or regularly can increase headache frequency and cause the transition from episodic to chronic headache."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes medication overuse headache Frequent use of acute headache medications (more than 10-15 days per month). Use of combination analgesics containing opioids, barbiturates, or caffeine. Pre-existing primary headache disorders, such as migraines or tension-type headaches. Psychological factors, such as anxiety or depression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequent use of acute headache medications (more than 10-15 days per month)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Use of combination analgesics containing opioids, barbiturates, or caffeine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pre-existing primary headache disorders, such as migraines or tension-type headaches."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Psychological factors, such as anxiety or depression."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequent use of acute headache medications (more than 10-15 days per month)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Use of combination analgesics containing opioids, barbiturates, or caffeine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pre-existing primary headache disorders, such as migraines or tension-type headaches."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Psychological factors, such as anxiety or depression."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Tension headache: Tension headache is the most common one. There may be a feeling of a band around the head or pressure at the vertex, and the pain is described as \"dull,\" \" tight,\" or as \"pressure.\""},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Chronic migraine: Migraine sufferers describe a prodrome of fatigue, agitation, or behavioural changes that last for a few hours or days. Typical migraine symptoms include a strong, throbbing headache, photophobia, phonophobia, and vomiting that lasts 4 to 72 hours."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Cluster headache: The 'cluster', which consists of runs of identical headaches starting at the same time and lasting for weeks at a time. Cluster headaches are notably periodic."}]},{"n":50,"q":"A 30-year-old man presented with excessive fatiguability towards the end of the day that improved with rest. He also gives history of ptosis, difficult in speech and swallowing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome"},{"t":"Duchenne muscular dystrophy"},{"t":"Systemic lupus erythematosus"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Myasthenia gravis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the patient's symptoms of fatiguability, ptosis, difficulty in speech and swallowing are characteristic of myasthenia gravis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features of myasthenia gravis Weakness, easy fatigability- cardinal features Diplopia Ptosis- Asymmetrical with diurnal variation Sustained upward gaze >30 secs causes triggering of ptosis Snarling fascies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness, easy fatigability- cardinal features"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diplopia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ptosis- Asymmetrical with diurnal variation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sustained upward gaze >30 secs causes triggering of ptosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Snarling fascies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weakness, easy fatigability- cardinal features"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diplopia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ptosis- Asymmetrical with diurnal variation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sustained upward gaze >30 secs causes triggering of ptosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Snarling fascies"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Lambert-Eaton syndrome: Lambert-Eaton syndrome (LES) is a rare autoimmune disorder characterized by muscle weakness and fatiguability. It is often associated with an underlying malignancy, particularly small cell lung cancer."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Duchenne muscular dystrophy: Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) is a genetic disorder characterized by progressive muscle weakness and wasting. It primarily affects boys and usually becomes evident in early childhood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE): Systemic lupus erythematosus is a chronic autoimmune disease that can affect multiple organ systems, including the musculoskeletal system. Muscle weakness and fatigue can occur in patients with SLE, but it is not the primary feature of the disease."}]},{"n":51,"q":"Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis is a complication of which of the following conditions?","o":[{"t":"Mumps"},{"t":"Measles"},{"t":"Rubella"},{"t":"RSV"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option B: Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis is a rare, chronic, progressive and eventually fatal complication of measles, presumably a result of an inability of the nervous system to eradicate the virus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A. Complications of mumps are expressed as inflammation of various organs such as orchitis, oophoritis, encephalitis, pancreatitis, etc."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. Complications of rubella include heart problems, loss of hearing and eyesight, intellectual disability, and liver or spleen damage."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D . The respiratory syncytial virus causes complications of the respiratory tract only."}]},{"n":52,"q":"What is the diagnosis of an elderly woman who is brought to the OPD with complaints of behavioral changes, a history of multiple falls, urinary incontinence, and dementia?","o":[{"t":"Normal pressure hydrocephalus"},{"t":"Frontotemporal dementia"},{"t":"Parkinson disease"},{"t":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NPH is characterized by the classic triad of gait disturbance (which can lead to falls), urinary incontinence, and cognitive impairment (dementia)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Behavioral changes can also be part of the cognitive impairment seen in NPH."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The condition is caused by an accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) in the brain ventricles without a significant increase in intracranial pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B . Frontotemporal dementia: FTD primarily affects personality, behavior, and language. It often presents with early changes in personality and behavior, such as disinhibition, apathy, and loss of empathy. Falls and urinary incontinence are not typical early features of FTD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C . Parkinson's disease: Parkinson's disease primarily presents with motor symptoms such as bradykinesia, resting tremor, rigidity, and postural instability. While dementia and falls can occur in the later stages of the disease, urinary incontinence is not a typical early feature, and the behavioral changes are usually associated with later stages or medication effects."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D . Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease: Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) is a rapidly progressive neurodegenerative disorder caused by prions. It is characterized by rapidly progressing dementia, myoclonus, ataxia, and other neurological signs. Behavioral changes can occur, but urinary incontinence and multiple falls are not typical presenting features."}]},{"n":53,"q":"What type of aphasia is observed in individuals with lesions in the posterior region of the superior temporal gyrus?","o":[{"t":"Fluent"},{"t":"Non fluent"},{"t":"Anomic"},{"t":"Conduction"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluent aphasias, also called receptive aphasias, indeed involve impairments mostly related to the input or reception of language."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wernicke's aphasia, a type of fluent aphasia, is characterized by difficulties in auditory verbal comprehension and the repetition of words, phrases, or sentences spoken by others."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The lesions causing Wernicke's aphasia are typically localized to the posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B. Non-fluent aphasia: Non-fluent aphasia, also known as Broca's aphasia, is associated with lesions in the frontal lobe, particularly the posterior part of the inferior frontal gyrus (Broca's area). Individuals with Broca's aphasia have difficulty with speech production but usually have relatively preserved comprehension."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. Anomic aphasia: Anomic aphasia is characterized by difficulty in finding words, particularly nouns and verbs, and is typically associated with lesions in various regions of the brain, including the temporal and parietal lobes. It is not specifically linked to the posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D. Conduction aphasia: Conduction aphasia is associated with lesions in the arcuate fasciculus, which is a white matter tract connecting Broca's area and Wernicke's area. Individuals with conduction aphasia typically have relatively preserved comprehension and speech production but have significant difficulty with repetition."}]},{"n":54,"q":"Identify the lesion shown in the image given below.","o":[{"t":"Port Wine stain"},{"t":"Melanoma"},{"t":"Molluscum contagiosum"},{"t":"Squamous cell carcinoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Port Wine stain:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Port Wine Stain is a phenomenon seen in children. It is a kind of hemangioma. It is usually present at birth and later fades with age. But it does not disappear. A hemangioma is, in fact, a vascular birthmark."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Melanoma is one of the most severe skin cancer. It is seen as a black-coloured mark on the skin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Molluscum contagiosum is a viral infection where the lesions are present in the form of dome-shaped pearly lesions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Squamous cell carcinoma is the cancer of the skin's outer and middle layer squamous cells."}],"qi":[8516]},{"n":55,"q":"All are true about Brown Sequard Syndrome except","o":[{"t":"I/L loss of vibration"},{"t":"I/L loss of joint position"},{"t":"C/L loss of pain"},{"t":"C/L loss of vibration"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option D."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral loss of vibration is not a characteristic feature of Brown-Sequard Syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral Proprioception loss Spastic weakness Development of radicular pain Fasciculation and areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proprioception loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spastic weakness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Development of radicular pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fasciculation and areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Contralateral Pain and temperature loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain and temperature loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proprioception loss"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Spastic weakness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Development of radicular pain"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fasciculation and areflexia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain and temperature loss"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, B, and C"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These are not a feature of Brown-Sequard Syndrome"}]},{"n":56,"q":"A lesion at which level produces incongruous homonymous hemianopia with wernicke’s pupil?","o":[{"t":"Optic tract"},{"t":"Visual cortex"},{"t":"Optic radiations"},{"t":"Optic nerve"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option: A"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incongruous homonymous hemianopia refers to a visual field defect where there is loss of vision on one side of the visual field in both eyes, but the defect does not match exactly between the eyes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wernicke's pupil, also known as an afferent pupillary defect or Marcus Gunn pupil, is a condition where there is a relative pupillary dilation in response to direct light stimulation in the affected eye. It indicates dysfunction of the afferent pathway carrying visual information from the retina to the brain."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B) Visual cortex: Lesions in the visual cortex, which is located in the occipital lobe at the back of the brain, can cause visual field defects, but they typically result in congruous homonymous hemianopia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C) Optic radiations: The optic radiations are nerve pathways that carry visual information from the visual cortex to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) in the thalamus. Lesions in the optic radiations can cause visual field defects, but they typically result in congruous hemianopia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D) Optic nerve: Lesions in the optic nerve, which connects the eye to the brain, can cause visual field defects, but they typically result in monocular vision loss (loss of vision in one eye) rather than a homonymous hemianopia."}]},{"n":57,"q":"At which level the lesion is located if all Extraocular muscles are paralyzed?","o":[{"t":"Frontal eye field"},{"t":"Cerebral surface"},{"t":"Midbrain and Pons"},{"t":"Spinal cord"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the midbrain and pons are affected the cranial nerves 3,4,5,6,7 and 8 are lost due to its location. The oculomotor nerve loses its control and hence extraocular movements are lost hence extra ocular muscles are paralyzed."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A : Frontal Eye Field is located in the brodmann area 8 controlled by the cerebellum which for horizontal gaze. It connects the extra ocular muscles with paramedian pontine reticular formation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B : Cerebral surface the cerebral cortex mainly deals with motor-sensory pathways and association areas."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D : Spinal Cord , a lesion/injury of the spinal cord care causes complete sensory and motor loss at the site of injury, compression or a shame here resulting in flaccid areflexic paralysis below the level of injury."}]},{"n":58,"q":"What is the preferred medication for the presented case? A male patient, 54 years old, was brought to the emergency room with symptoms of fever, headache, and a focal seizure. The EEG results indicate the presence of spikes in the temporal lobe. Analysis of the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) sample reveals the presence of 50 cells, with 90% lymphocytes and 10% neutrophils.","o":[{"t":"Valacyclovir"},{"t":"Acyclovir"},{"t":"Ganciclovir"},{"t":"Foscarnet"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Focal temporal lobe spikes along with symptoms of fever, headache and seizures indicate HSV meningitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The empirical therapy of HSV is Acyclovir."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Valacyclovir is indicated in Varicella Zoster associated meningitis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Ganciclovir is mainly considered for cytomegalovirus associated meningitis and can be sometimes supportively added with acyclovir in HSV."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Foscarnet is the drug of choice for immunocompromised cytomegalovirus meningitis in drug-resistant ganciclovir cases."}]},{"n":59,"q":"A patient comes with a blood pressure of 160/100 mmHg and left-sided facial paralysis and weakness since 8 hrs. Her CT appears normal. What would be your next step?","o":[{"t":"Observation and monitoring"},{"t":"Intravenous labetalol"},{"t":"Intravenous thrombolysis"},{"t":"Oral Nifedipine"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This patient may be experiencing a transient ischemic attack or an ischemic stroke if they have sudden onset hemiparesis and a normal CT scan. For this patient, observation and monitoring would be the next course of action."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The neurological symptoms usually resolve within 24 hours, but if the symptoms persist for >24 hours it is evolving in to stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B : Labetalol is used in the acute phase of stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C : Intravenous thrombolysis this should be done only after confirmation of ischemic stroke in the patient and should be initiated within 4.5 hours."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D : Oral Nifedipine can be an antihypertensive agent used in the acute phase of stroke."}]},{"n":60,"q":"A 20 year old male was brought for psychiatric evaluation for intellectual disability. On examination, his IQ was 45. He has a long face, large ears, large testis and a cardiac murmur and A history of poor intellectual function is present in the paternal family. What is his diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Cushing’s syndrome"},{"t":"Rett syndrome"},{"t":"Fragile X syndrome"},{"t":"William syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Fragile X syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case is that of Fragile X as the patient has a long face, large ears, large testis, cardiac murmur and a history of poor intellectual function present in the paternal family ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is the second most common cause of mental retardation. Fragile X syndrome is an X-linked dominant disease due to CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Cushing syndrome presents with acne, hirsutism, thin skin, delayed wound healing, anxiety, irritability, depression are seen along with involvement of musculoskeletal, endocrine and metabolic systems."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Rett syndrome presents with autistic-like behaviors, breathing irregularities, feeding and swallowing difficulties, growth retardation, and seizures."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Williams syndrome is characterized by mild to moderate intellectual disability or learning problems, unique personality characteristics, distinctive facial features, and heart and blood vessel (cardiovascular) problems."}]},{"n":61,"q":"What is the treatment of Grade I Meningioma?","o":[{"t":"Reassurance"},{"t":"Surgical excision"},{"t":"Chemotherapy"},{"t":"Radiotherapy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Meningiomas are the most common benign tumors seen in adults and originate from the meningothelial cells of the arachnoid."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The treatment of grade 1 meningioma is surgical excision."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A : All meningioma need treatment. Reassurance is not enough."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C : Chemotherapy is not used for meningiomas."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D : Radiotherapy is employed in grade 2 and grade 3."}]},{"n":62,"q":"What should be the next course of action in the management of a 50-year-old male who experienced sudden onset of right-sided weakness and aphasia within a two-hour timeframe, with a recorded blood pressure of 160/110mmHg and a clear non-contrast computed tomography (NCCT) scan?","o":[{"t":"CT angiography to look for large vessel occlusion"},{"t":"MRI to look for infarct"},{"t":"Tab Labetalol 10 mg stat"},{"t":"Thrombolysis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A - CT angiography to look for large vessel occlusion:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The next step in management would be CT Angiography."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NCCT and CT Angiography must be done concurrently during stroke evaluation. The given features of hemiparesis with aphasia and a clear NCCT are suggestive of an acute ischemic stroke with a proximal large vessel occlusion. Therefore the patient should undergo immediate CT Angiography to localize."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - MRI to look for infarct: MRI is not used in the diagnosis of stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tab Labetalol 10 mg stat: ACE inhibitors/diuretics are used in BP control in stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Thrombolysis: Thrombolysis can be done in this patient but CT angiography is done to confirm the occlusion."}]},{"n":63,"q":"What is the preferred treatment for relieving symptoms during an acute episode of cluster headache?","o":[{"t":"Oral sumatriptan"},{"t":"Subcutaneous sumatriptan"},{"t":"100% oxygen at 6 L/minute"},{"t":"100% oxygen at 8 L/minute"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: Option B:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sumatriptan is a medication from the triptan class that is commonly used to treat acute migraine and cluster headaches."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dose of Sumatriptan- 6mg subcutaneous"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Oral sumatriptan may not provide the same rapid relief as other forms of administration, such as subcutaneous injection."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C, and D: High flow oxygen at 12-15 L/min decreases the severity of the pain."}]},{"n":64,"q":"Which of the following is not considered a primary diagnostic factor for neuromyelitis optica?","o":[{"t":"Area postrema syndrome"},{"t":"Acute myelitis"},{"t":"Focal epilepsy"},{"t":"Optic neuritis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option C: Focal epilepsy-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuromyelitis optica (NMO), also known as Devic’s disease, is an autoimmune inflammatory disorder that primarily affects the optic nerves and spinal cord."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Focal epilepsy is not a major diagnostic criteria for Neuromyelitis optica"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Area postrema syndrome: This refers to symptoms related to inflammation of the area postrema, a region in the brainstem involved in regulating vomiting. Symptoms may include intractable hiccups, nausea, and vomiting."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Acute myelitis: This involves inflammation of the spinal cord, resulting in neurological symptoms such as limb weakness, sensory loss, and bladder/bowel dysfunction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Optic neuritis: Inflammation of the optic nerves leading to visual impairment, often characterized by pain behind the eyes and vision loss."}]},{"n":65,"q":"Which of the following does not show hypokinesia movements?","o":[{"t":"Parkinson’s disease"},{"t":"Apraxia"},{"t":"Catatonia"},{"t":"Chorea"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Chorea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chorea is a movement disorder that causes sudden, unintended, and uncontrollable jerky movements of the arms, legs, and facial muscles ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is caused by an overactivity of dopamine in the areas of the brain that control movement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Parkinson’s disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The cardinal features of Parkinson’s disease include bradykinesia (slowness of movement), rigidity (stiffness), resting tremors, and postural instability."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Apraxia-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Apraxia is the loss of ability to execute or carry out skilled movement and gestures, despite having the physical ability and desire to perform them ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Catatonia-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Catatonia, a neuropsychiatric syndrome characterized by abnormal movements, behaviors, and withdrawal , is a condition that is most often seen in mood disorders but can also be seen in psychotic, medical, neurologic, and other disorders"}]},{"n":66,"q":"Which of the following statements regarding electroencephalogram (EEG) is not correct?\n(or)\nWhich of the following statements regarding electroencephalogram (EEG) is correct?","o":[{"t":"1-5% of the population can have epileptiform discharges"},{"t":"Doing EEG is mandatory for diagnosis of seizures"},{"t":"Scalp EEG may be helpful in localizing frontal lobe epilepsy"},{"t":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy shows triphasic and slow waves on EEG"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option B: Doing EEG is mandatory for the diagnosis of seizures-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The statement that is NOT correct among the options provided."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis of seizures is often based on a combination of clinical history, physical examination, and sometimes additional diagnostic tests."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EEG is particularly useful when there is uncertainty about the nature of events or when further information is needed to guide treatment."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: 1-5% of the population can have epileptiform discharges-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is correct. Epileptiform discharges on EEG can be found in a percentage of the population without a history of clinical seizures."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Scalp EEG may be helpful in localizing frontal lobe epilepsy-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is correct. Scalp EEG can be valuable in localizing the origin of epileptic activity in the brain, including identifying specific lobe involvement such as the frontal lobe."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy shows focal or multifocal abnormalities-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is correct. EEG findings in PML can vary but often show focal or multifocal abnormalities rather than a specific triphasic pattern."}]},{"n":67,"q":"A 45-year-old male patient presented with a history of bilateral lower limb weakness, which progressed to his upper limbs in a year. On examination, he had weakness in both lower limbs and wasting in the left upper limb. Babinski sign was positive and the deep tendon reflexes were absent. He has no sensory loss or any autonomic dysfunction. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"MS"},{"t":"ALS"},{"t":"GBS"},{"t":"Tropical spastic paraparesis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B: ALS"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Combination of Primary muscular atrophy and Primary lateral sclerosis is called Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary muscular atrophy- Lower Motor Neuron of the spinal cord, Brain stem cranial nerve nuclei causing Bulbar palsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary lateral sclerosis- Pyramidal Tract 2nd corticobulbar fibers innervating, Brain stem cranial nerve nuclei is UMN damage. Pseudobulbar palsy Exaggerated gag reflex Balbinski sign Brisk Deep Tendon Reflexes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pseudobulbar palsy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exaggerated gag reflex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Balbinski sign"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brisk Deep Tendon Reflexes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pseudobulbar palsy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Exaggerated gag reflex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Balbinski sign"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brisk Deep Tendon Reflexes"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option a: Multiple Sclerosis (MS) typically presents with a relapsing-remitting course and involves various neurological symptoms, including sensory loss, autonomic dysfunction, and optic neuritis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option c: Guillain-Barré Syndrome (GBS) typically presents with absent deep tendon reflexes, but it is less likely in this case as the weakness started in the upper limbs."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option d: Tropical spastic paraparesis primarily affects the lower limbs, causing weakness and spasticity."}]},{"n":68,"q":"In a patient who is 66 years old and has a history of chronic hypertension, he arrives at the emergency room with changes in mental state and high blood pressure measuring 210/110 mm Hg. A brain CT scan shows bleeding within the brain tissue. The typical location of this bleeding in similar cases is known as the:","o":[{"t":"Putamen"},{"t":"Thalamus"},{"t":"Pons"},{"t":"Cerebellum"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A: Putamen-"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical scenario, the most common site of hemorrhage in cases of intraparenchymal hemorrhage with chronic hypertension is the Putamen. The correct answer is: Putamen"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B, C, and D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While intraparenchymal hemorrhage can occur in other brain regions, such as the Thalamus, Pons, or Cerebellum, the Putamen is the most commonly affected site in cases of chronic hypertension-associated hemorrhage."}]},{"n":69,"q":"A 60 year old woman presents with weakness in right arm for 6 hours of duration. The weakness gradually became lesser and resolves. She is a known case of diabetes and hypertension and is obese. Which of the following is the probable diagnosis of this case?","o":[{"t":"Compressive neuropathy"},{"t":"Transient ischaemic attack"},{"t":"Ischaemic stroke"},{"t":"Diabetic neuropathy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option: B"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical presentation, the probable diagnosis in this case is Transient Ischaemic Attack (TIA)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurological symptoms begin to resolve within 1 hour and the cutoff for symptoms of TIA to resolve is 24 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The anti-clotting system causes spontaneous lysis of clot causing resolution of symptoms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Compressive neuropathy typically results in persistent symptoms rather than transient symptoms as described in the case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Ischaemic stroke involves a more prolonged disruption of blood flow to a specific area of the brain, leading to persistent neurological deficits that do not resolve within 24 hours."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Diabetic neuropathy is unlikely to cause transient weakness limited to one side of the body, as seen in this case."}]},{"n":70,"q":"What is the GCS of a patient with a head injury who opens her eyes in response to a loud voice, localizes pain and is confused and disoriented but can answer questions?","o":[{"t":"E3 M5 V4"},{"t":"E3 M6 V5"},{"t":"E4 M4 V5"},{"t":"E3 M5 V5"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A: E3 M5 V4 -"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Eye opening response (E3): Eyes open to verbal command, speech or shout"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motor response (M5): Purposeful movement to painful stimulus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Verbal response (V4): Confused and disoriented"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the overall GCS score for the patient is E3M5V4."}]},{"n":71,"q":"Which of the following combinations of seizure and its corresponding medication is not accurate? Generalized onset tonic – Clonic: Lamotrigine Focal seizures: Levetiracetam Typical absence seizures: Topiramate Myoclonic: Valporic acid","o":[{"t":"1"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"4"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 3:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Topiramate is not typically used as a first-line medication for typical absence seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The drug of choice for typical absence seizures is valproate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ethosuximide is used in children <2 years as valproate has higher chances of fulminant hepatic failure as the age lowers."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Lamotrigine is a commonly used medication for generalized onset tonic-clonic seizures, as well as other seizure types."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Levetiracetam is an effective medication for focal seizures and is widely used as a first-line treatment for this seizure type."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Valproic acid (Sodium valproate) is commonly used for the treatment of various seizure types, including myoclonic seizures."}]},{"n":72,"q":"A 30 year old man presents with distal leg weakness, reduced muscle stretch reflexes, with no complaints of tingling or numbness. On examination, there is atrophy of the muscles below the knee. The following clinical findings are seen and similar history is seen in elder brother. The most probable diagnosis is—","o":[{"t":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease"},{"t":"Refsum’s disease"},{"t":"Neuropathy associated with Sjogren’s syndrome"},{"t":"Rheumatoid arthritis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease is an umbrella for inherited neuropathies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common CMT is the autosomal dominantly inherited CMT type 1. usually caused by a mutation in the PMP-22."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Common signs are distal wasting (inverted champagne bottle legs), often with pes cavus, and predominantly motor involvement."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patient with CMT do not complain of tingling or numbness"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Refsum disease, also known as heredopathia atactica polyneuritiformis cause a range of symptoms, including peripheral neuropathy, retinitis pigmentosa, hearing loss, balance issues, muscle weakness, and skin changes. Anosmia, or loss of smell, is a notable feature of Refsum disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Sjögren's syndrome is a chronic autoimmune disorder that primarily affects the moisture-producing glands in the body, leading to symptoms of dryness in the eyes and mouth."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a chronic autoimmune disease that primarily affects the joints, causing inflammation, pain, stiffness, and swelling."}],"qi":[8528]},{"n":73,"q":"Which of the subsequent options is not a viable treatment choice for Guillain Barre syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Steroids"},{"t":"Plasmapheresis"},{"t":"IVIG"},{"t":"Ventilator support"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Steroids:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucocorticoids have not been found to be effective in the treatment of Guillain-Barre syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Plasmapheresis: Plasmapheresis is not the treatment of choice, but it is equally effective and functional improvement occurs after 1 week."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - IVIG (intravenous immunoglobulin): IVIG is the treatment of choice in GBS because of its ease of administration and safety profile."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ventilator support: Ventilator support is required in severe cases of GBS."}]},{"n":74,"q":"According to the ABCD2 scoring, which factors indicate a greater likelihood of a patient with TIA experiencing a future stroke?","o":[{"t":"Age <60 years"},{"t":"SBP>140 mm Hg and DBP <90 mmHg"},{"t":"Duration of TIA >5 mins"},{"t":"Diabetes"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Diabetes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Presence of diabetes indicates higher risk of stroke following TIA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ABCD2 score: Age >60 years- 1 point BP >140/90 mmHg- 1 point Clinical features Arm weakness- 1 point Speech impairment- 1 point Duration of symptoms 10-59 mins- 1 point >60 mins- 2 points Diabetes mellitus- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age >60 years- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BP >140/90 mmHg- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features Arm weakness- 1 point Speech impairment- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arm weakness- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Speech impairment- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duration of symptoms 10-59 mins- 1 point >60 mins- 2 points"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"10-59 mins- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":">60 mins- 2 points"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes mellitus- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age >60 years- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"BP >140/90 mmHg- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features Arm weakness- 1 point Speech impairment- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arm weakness- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Speech impairment- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duration of symptoms 10-59 mins- 1 point >60 mins- 2 points"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"10-59 mins- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":">60 mins- 2 points"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetes mellitus- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arm weakness- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Speech impairment- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"10-59 mins- 1 point"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":">60 mins- 2 points"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Max. score- 7 will have 22% chances of developing stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Age <60 years: Age >60 years is risk factor of developing stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - SBP >140 mm Hg and DBP <90 mmHg: Blood pressure >140/90 mmHg is a risk factor for developing stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Duration of TIA >5 mins: Duration of symptoms for 10-59 mins is a risk factor for stroke."}]},{"n":75,"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding Guillain-Barre syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Ascending paralysis"},{"t":"Descending paralysis is seen"},{"t":"Plasmapheresis is a treatment method"},{"t":"Demyelinating disorder"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Descending paralysis is seen :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) is an autoimmune polyradiculopathy causing patchy segmental demyelination of spinal cord and peripheral nerves."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Type of paralysisin GBS- Ascending symmetrical paralysis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Ascending paralysis : GBS typically presents as ascending paralysis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Plasmapheresis is a treatment method: Plasmapheresis, also known as plasma exchange, is one of the treatment options for GBS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Demyelinating disorder: GBS is causes patchy segmental demyelination of spinal cord and peripheral nerves."}]},{"n":76,"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding patients with trigeminal neuralgia?","o":[{"t":"More common in females"},{"t":"Pain along V2 and V3 division of trigeminal nerve"},{"t":"Deep seated pain"},{"t":"No objective signs of sensory loss"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Deep-seated Pain:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Trigeminal neuralgia does not present with deep-seated pain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pain in trigeminal neuralgia is repetitive, severe, and very brief (seconds or less). It may be triggered by touch, a cold wind or eating."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - More common in females: Trigeminal neuralgia is more commonly seen in females, although it can affect both sexes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Pain along V2 and V3 division of trigeminal nerve: The lancinating pain of trigeminal neuralgia most commonly involves the second and/or third division of trigeminal nerve."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - No objective signs of sensory loss : Patients with trigeminal neuralgia do not exhibit objective sensory loss in the affected areas. The pain is usually paroxysmal and does not cause long-term sensory deficits."}]},{"n":77,"q":"What is the most frequently observed form of Guillain-Barre syndrome among the options provided?","o":[{"t":"Acute motor axonal neuropathy"},{"t":"Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy"},{"t":"Acute motor sensory axonal neuropathy"},{"t":"Miller fisher syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP) is the most common subtype of GBS seen mostly in adults."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It has motor, sensory, and autonomic involvement."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN): This is a subtype of Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) characterized by axonal damage to motor nerves. It is less common than AIDP."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acute motor sensory axonal neuropathy (AMSAN ): This is another subtype of GBS characterized by axonal damage to both motor and sensory nerves. It is less common than AIDP."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Miller Fisher syndrome (MFS) : This is a variant of GBS characterized by a triad of symptoms: ataxia , ophthalmoplegia , and areflexia . It is less common than AIDP."}]},{"n":78,"q":"What is the consequence of thrombosis in the posterior inferior cerebellar artery?","o":[{"t":"Lateral medullary syndrome"},{"t":"Weber syndrome"},{"t":"Medial medullary syndrome"},{"t":"Millard Gubler syndrome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lateral medullary syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombosis of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) can cause lateral medullary syndrome, also known as Wallenberg syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lateral medullary syndrome can occur due to occlusion of any of the 5 vessels Vertebral artery Posterior inferior cerebellar artery Superior medullary artery Middle medullary artery Inferior lateral medullary artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vertebral artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superior medullary artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Middle medullary artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inferior lateral medullary artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It presents with vertigo, numbness of the ipsilateral face and contralateral limbs, diplopia, hoarseness, dysarthria, dysphagia, and ipsilateral Horner’s syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Vertebral artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Superior medullary artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Middle medullary artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Inferior lateral medullary artery"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Weber syndrome: This statement is incorrect. Weber syndrome is caused by the lesions in the midbrain."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Medial medullary syndrome: This statement is incorrect. Medial medullary syndrome is caused by the thrombosis of the anterior spinal artery."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Millard Gubler syndrome: This statement is incorrect. Millard Gubler syndrome, also known as ventral pontine syndrome , is caused by a lesion affecting the pons."}]},{"n":79,"q":"A 33-year-old female came complains of weakness, easy fatigability, double vision and drooping of the upper eyelids. On examination her deep tendon reflexes are normal. What is the pathogenesis of the condition in this case?","o":[{"t":"Decreased acetylcholine release at the nerve endings"},{"t":"Decreased myosin"},{"t":"Absence of troponin C"},{"t":"Decreased synaptic transmission at the myoneural junction"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Decreased synaptic transmission at the myoneural junction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's symptoms—weakness, easy fatigability, double vision (diplopia), and drooping of the upper eyelids (ptosis)—along with normal deep tendon reflexes, suggest a diagnosis of myasthenia gravis . The pathogenesis of myasthenia gravis involves an autoimmune attack on the acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In myasthenia gravis, autoantibodies target and destroy acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. This decreases the efficiency of synaptic transmission, leading to muscle weakness and fatigue, particularly after repeated use."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Decreased acetylcholine release at the nerve endings: This option is incorrect. In myasthenia gravis, the primary defect lies in the postsynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction, where autoantibodies target and interfere with AChRs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Decreased myosin: This option is incorrect. Myosin is a protein involved in muscle contraction. Myasthenia gravis does not involve a decrease in myosin levels or dysfunction of myosin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Absence of troponin C: This option is incorrect. Troponin C is a component of the regulatory complex involved in muscle contraction. Its absence is not associated with myasthenia gravis."}]},{"n":80,"q":"Which of the following statements about Weber's syndrome is incorrect?","o":[{"t":"Contralateral hemiplegia"},{"t":"Ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy"},{"t":"Contralateral parkinsonism"},{"t":"Ipsilateral paralysis of lower face"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Ipsilateral paralysis of lower face:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Weber's syndrome typically does not involve paralysis of the lower face."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy, or paralysis of the facial muscles on the same side as the brain lesion, such as Bell's palsy or lesions affecting the facial nerve itself."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Contralateral hemiplegia: Weber's syndrome, also known as midbrain syndrome, is characterized by the presence of contralateral hemiplegia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy: Weber's syndrome is associated with ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Contralateral parkinsonism: Weber's syndrome is commonly associated with contralateral parkinsonism."}]},{"n":81,"q":"What is the typical pattern observed in Brown-Sequard syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Contralateral loss of joint sense and position"},{"t":"Contralateral loss of pain sensation"},{"t":"Ipsilateral loss of complete sensory functions"},{"t":"Contralateral motor functions"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Contralateral loss of pain sensation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The characteristic pattern seen in Brown-Sequard syndrome is contralateral loss of pain sensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brown-Sequard syndrome is a neurological condition caused by damage to one half of the spinal cord."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, C, and D: These options are incorrect"}]},{"n":82,"q":"What medication is employed for mass chemoprophylaxis in the case of meningococcal meningitis?","o":[{"t":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"t":"Chloramphenicol"},{"t":"Tetracycline"},{"t":"Penicillin"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Ciprofloxacin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ciprofloxacin is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that is effective against Neisseria meningitidis. It is the drug of choice for mass chemoprophylaxis in individuals who have been in close contact with a person diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ciprofloxacin is effective in eradicating the bacteria from the nasopharynx, reducing the risk of transmission."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ciprofloxacin is contraindicated in 1 st trimester of pregnancy, hence ceftriaxone is given."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B- Chloramphenicol: Chloramphenicol can be used for the treatment of meningococcal meningitis, it is not the preferred choice for mass chemoprophylaxis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C- Tetracycline: Tetracycline is not the drug of choice for mass chemoprophylaxis.."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D- Penicillin: Penicillin is effective against many strains of Neisseria meningitidis, but it is not a preferred drug for mass prophylaxis."}]},{"n":83,"q":"A patient with a history of a backache for 10 days, has now presented with sudden onset difficulty in micturition and defaecation. There was no history of a cough or fever previously. What is the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pott's Spine"},{"t":"Gullian Barre Syndrome"},{"t":"Cauda Equina Syndrome"},{"t":"Multiple Sclerosis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cauda Equina Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cauda Equina Syndrome is a medical emergency characterized by the compression of the cauda equina nerve roots in the lower spinal canal."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features of cauda equina syndrome: Difficulty with micturition Loss of anal sphincter tone or fecal incontinence Saddle anesthesia Gait disturbance Pain, numbness or weakness affecting one or both legs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty with micturition"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of anal sphincter tone or fecal incontinence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Saddle anesthesia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gait disturbance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain, numbness or weakness affecting one or both legs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Difficulty with micturition"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Loss of anal sphincter tone or fecal incontinence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Saddle anesthesia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gait disturbance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pain, numbness or weakness affecting one or both legs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pott's Spine: Pott's Spine, also known as spinal tuberculosis, typically presents with chronic back pain, deformity, and constitutional symptoms such as weight loss and fever."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Guillain-Barré Syndrome: Guillain-Barré Syndrome is an autoimmune condition characterized by ascending muscle weakness, usually starting in the legs and progressing upwards."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Multiple Sclerosis: Multiple Sclerosis (MS) presents with a wide range of neurological symptoms, including muscle weakness, sensory changes, and coordination problems."}]},{"n":84,"q":"A 16-year-old girl, who is taking antiepileptics, has had a seizure-free period of 6 months. She has no family history of epilepsy. Her EEG is now normal and she has a normal neurological exam and intelligence. What would your advice be?","o":[{"t":"Stop the treatment and follow up"},{"t":"Gradually taper the drug and stop treatment"},{"t":"Continue treatment for another 2 years"},{"t":"Continue lifelong treatment with antiepileptics"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Continue treatment for another 2 years:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The criteria for seizure free after drug withdrawal: Complete medical control of seizures for 1-5 years. Single seizure type, with generalized seizures having a better prognosis that focal seizures. Normal neurologic examination, including intelligence No family history of epilepsy Normal EEG."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complete medical control of seizures for 1-5 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Single seizure type, with generalized seizures having a better prognosis that focal seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal neurologic examination, including intelligence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No family history of epilepsy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal EEG."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The treatment needs to be continued for 2 years as the risk of seizure recurrence after a seizure free period is highest within first 2 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complete medical control of seizures for 1-5 years."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Single seizure type, with generalized seizures having a better prognosis that focal seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal neurologic examination, including intelligence"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No family history of epilepsy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal EEG."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Stop the treatment and follow up: Stopping the treatment abruptly in a patient with a history of epilepsy, even after a seizure-free period, carries a risk of seizure recurrence."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Gradually taper the drug and stop treatment: Tapering and stopping the drug immediately after a 6-month seizure-free period may increase the risk of seizure recurrence."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Continue lifelong treatment with antiepileptics: Lifelong treatment with AEDs is not necessary in all cases of epilepsy."}]},{"n":85,"q":"A patient presents with left-sided facial paralysis and weakness for the past 1 hour. Her blood pressure is 160/100 mmHg and CT appears normal. What would be your next step?","o":[{"t":"Nothing, since CT was normal"},{"t":"Start on aspirin + clopidogrel"},{"t":"Intravenous thrombolysis"},{"t":"Advice BP control"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Intravenous thrombolysis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presentation of left-sided facial paralysis and weakness in a patient with high blood pressure suggests a possible ischemic stroke involving the territory of the middle cerebral artery."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrmobloysis is the preferred treatment in this patient as there is no hemorrhagic stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Window period for thrombolysis is <4.5 hours of symptoms onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombolysis can be done up to 6 hours of symptom onset."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option Options :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Nothing , since CT was normal: While the CT scan may not show any immediate abnormalities, it does not rule out an acute ischemic stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Start on aspirin + clopidogrel: Dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and clopidogrel is commonly used for the prevention of recurrent strokes in patients with ischemic stroke of atherosclerotic origin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Advice BP control: Blood pressure control is an essential aspect of managing acute ischemic stroke, but in this scenario, the immediate concern is the potential for thrombolysis."}]},{"n":86,"q":"A patient walks with a stomping gait. When asked to close his eyes and walk, he is unable to do so. Which of the following tracts is probably affected?","o":[{"t":"Spinocerebellar tract"},{"t":"Posterior column tract"},{"t":"Vestibulospinal tract"},{"t":"Rubrospinal tract"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Posterior column tract:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A stomping gait, also known as a sensory ataxia, is typically due to loss of proprioception, which is the sense of the relative position of one's own body parts. Proprioception is mainly carried by the posterior column (dorsal column) of the spinal cord."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: The posterior column tract (or dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway) carries proprioceptive information, vibration sense, and fine touch."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to this tract results in loss of proprioception, causing a stomping gait as the patient relies on visual cues to know the position of their legs. When eyes are closed, the patient loses these cues, resulting in difficulty walking."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Spinocerebellar tract"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: Carries unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum to help coordinate movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to this tract results in ataxia, but it is usually characterized by uncoordinated movements rather than a stomping gait. The patient might not show increased difficulty with eyes closed compared to eyes open."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Vestibulospinal tract"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: Involved in maintaining balance and posture by controlling the muscles of the trunk and limbs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Damage to this tract would result in balance issues, but the patient would not typically present with a stomping gait or show significant worsening with eyes closed."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Rubrospinal tract"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Function: Involved in the fine control of limb movements, particularly the flexors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reasoning: Lesions in this tract are rare and typically do not cause a stomping gait or the described difficulty in walking with eyes closed. They might cause more subtle motor deficits."}]},{"n":87,"q":"In the sagittal section of the brain given below, if the coloured area is affected, which of the following is not seen?","o":[{"t":"Urinary incontinence"},{"t":"Gait apraxia"},{"t":"Perianal anesthesia"},{"t":"Fecal incontinence"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Perianal anesthesia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The sagittal section of the brain shown in the image likely represents a lesion in the area of the cerebral cortex known as the precentral gyrus or primary motor cortex. This region is responsible for motor control and coordination."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Perianal anesthesia: Perianal anesthesia refers to the loss of sensation around the anus. The precentral gyrus, which is primarily involved in motor function, is not directly responsible for sensory perception. Sensory information, including perianal sensation, is processed in the somatosensory cortex, which is located in the postcentral gyrus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Urinary incontinence: The precentral gyrus is involved in the control of voluntary movement, including the coordination of the muscles involved in urinary continence. Damage to this area can disrupt the normal control of urinary function, leading to urinary incontinence."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Gait apraxia: The precentral gyrus is crucial for the initiation and coordination of voluntary movements, including walking. Therefore, damage to this area can result in gait apraxia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Fecal incontinence: The precentral gyrus is involved in the coordination of the muscles involved in maintaining bowel continence. Damage to this area can disrupt the normal control of bowel function, leading to fecal incontinence."}],"qi":[8538]},{"n":88,"q":"What is the recommended treatment for a patient who has a severe history of a road traffic accident and exhibits signs of elevated intracranial pressure upon examination?","o":[{"t":"Administer antibiotics for potential infection."},{"t":"Initiate anticoagulant therapy to prevent blood clot formation."},{"t":"Perform immediate surgical intervention."},{"t":"Administer mannitol"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Administer mannitol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Administer mannitol to reduce intracranial pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Management of raised ICP Head end elevation to improve the jugular venous flow. Osmotherapy with mannitol/3% saline/ Glycerol/Acetazolamide (IV)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Head end elevation to improve the jugular venous flow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Osmotherapy with mannitol/3% saline/ Glycerol/Acetazolamide (IV)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Head end elevation to improve the jugular venous flow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Osmotherapy with mannitol/3% saline/ Glycerol/Acetazolamide (IV)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A . Administering antibiotics would be appropriate if there is evidence of an infection, but it may not directly address the raised intracranial pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B . Initiating anticoagulant therapy may be indicated for certain conditions (e.g., deep vein thrombosis)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. Immediate surgical intervention may be necessary in some cases, such as for the evacuation of an intracranial hematoma or for decompressive craniectomy."}]},{"n":89,"q":"In hemiplegia caused by an ischemic stroke, which region of the internal capsule is impacted?","o":[{"t":"Retrolentiform"},{"t":"Sublentiform"},{"t":"Anterior limb"},{"t":"Posterior limb"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Posterior limb:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When an ischemic stroke occurs in the area supplied by the blood vessels surrounding the posterior limb of the internal capsule, the motor fibers within this region can be damaged."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This results in hemiplegia on the opposite side of the body from the affected internal capsule."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Retrolentiform: This term is not anatomically accurate and does not correspond to a specific part of the internal capsule."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Sublentiform: Similar to option A, this term is not anatomically accurate and does not correspond to a specific part of the internal capsule."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Anterior limb: Damage to the anterior limb can cause different symptoms but is not typically associated with hemiplegia."}]},{"n":90,"q":"Which artery is involved in a patient who presents with vertigo, diplopia, hoarseness, dysphagia, left Horner's syndrome, and numbness in the left face and right side limbs?","o":[{"t":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"t":"Superior cerebellar artery"},{"t":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"t":"Basilar artery"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Posterior inferior cerebellar artery:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wallenberg syndrome presents with pain, numbness, impaired sensation over one half of the face, horner syndrome, diplopia, numbness of the ipsilateral arm, trunk and leg."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebral artery is responsible for Wallenberg syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PICA supplies the lateral medulla."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Superior cerebellar artery: The superior cerebellar artery supplies the superior cerebellar hemisphere, midbrain, and parts of the cerebellar nuclei."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Anterior inferior cerebellar artery: The anterior inferior cerebellar artery supplies the anterior inferior portion of the cerebellum, the lateral pons, and parts of the internal auditory canal."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Basilar artery: The basilar artery is formed by the fusion of the vertebral arteries and supplies various structures in the brainstem, including the pons, midbrain, and cerebellum."}]},{"n":91,"q":"Which of the subsequent statements accurately describe tremors? PD is characterized by resting tremor Tremor consist of alternate contraction of agonist and antagonist muscle in an oscillating rhythmic manner Essential tremor is an uncommon movement disorder affecting 5% of the population Normal individuals can have physiologic tremors that manifest as mild high frequency, postural or action tremors","o":[{"t":"1,2,3 only"},{"t":"2,3,4 only"},{"t":"1,2,4 only"},{"t":"All of the above"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 1,2,4 only:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"True statements about tremors are:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PD is characterized by resting tremor:Resting tremor is a hallmark feature of Parkinson's disease (PD). Tremor consists of alternate contraction of agonist and antagonist muscles in an oscillating rhythmic manner: Tremors involve rhythmic oscillations of body parts and are typically caused by alternating contractions of agonist and antagonist muscles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal individuals can have physiologic tremors that manifest as mild high frequency, postural, or action tremors : Physiologic tremors are normal variations in hand or body tremors that can occur in healthy individuals. They are typically mild and high frequency,occurring during certain postures or actions such as holding a position against gravity orduring fine motor tasks."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect statement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Essential tremor is an uncommon movement disorder affecting 5% of the population: Essential tremor is actually a common movement disorder, around 5% to 10% of individuals, especially in older age groups. Essential tremor is characterized by postural or action tremors, often involving the hands, head, or voice."}]},{"n":92,"q":"A 37-year-old man is brought to the emergency room by his son. He complains of being unable to move his left arm and leg. According to his son, the patient was reading a book half an hour ago when he realized he couldn't lift his left arm to pick up another book. As he tried to move away from the table, he noticed that his left leg also became very weak. Additionally, he experienced double vision. The patient has a history of hypertension and hyperlipidemia. His body temperature is 36.1°C (97°F), blood pressure is 165/100 mmHg, pulse is 85 beats/min, and he has a respiratory rate of 16 breaths/min. During the examination, it is observed that the patient has drooping of the right eyelid and his right eye is directed downwards and to the side. When requested to smile, there is a drooping of the left corner of the mouth. The strength of the left upper and lower limbs is rated as 3 out of 5, whereas the strength of the right upper and lower limbs is rated as 5 out of 5. Which region of his brain has experienced ischemia among the following options?","o":[{"t":"Inner capsule"},{"t":"Medulla"},{"t":"Midbrain"},{"t":"Occipital lobe"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Midbrain:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's clinical presentation is consistent with a lacunar infarct, which is a small, localized ischemic stroke that occurs in the deep structures of the brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Midbrain, also known as the mesencephalon, is a region of the brainstem that plays a role in coordinating motor movements and controlling eye movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the patient's symptoms, including weakness on the left side of the body and abnormal eye movements (ptosis, downward and lateral deviation of the right eye), suggest an ischemic event affecting the midbrain ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Inner capsule- Damage to the inner capsule can cause motor and sensory deficits. However, in this case, the patient's symptoms are not consistent with an infarct in the inner capsule."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Medulla- While a stroke in the medulla can cause various neurological deficits, the patient's symptoms, such as weakness in the left arm and leg and double vision, are not specific to a medullary infarct."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Occipital lobe- Patient’s visual symptoms of double vision are more likely related to the involvement of the midbrain and its control over eye movements rather than a specific occipital lobe infarct"}]},{"n":93,"q":"What is the most common cause of Berry aneurysm?","o":[{"t":"Endothelial injury of vessel due to HTN"},{"t":"Muscle intimal elastic lamina layer defect"},{"t":"Endothelial layer defect"},{"t":"Adventitia defect"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B – Muscle intimal elastic lamina layer defect:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Internal elastic lamina layers of blood vessel is first affected, decreasing the ability of the blood vessels to respond to the tensile shearing stress and making the aneurysm vulnerable to rupture."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - is incorrect because while hypertension (HTN) can contribute to the weakening of blood vessels, it is not the primary cause of berry aneurysms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - is incorrect because it mentions an endothelial layer defect, which is not the primary cause of berry aneurysms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - is incorrect because adventitia defect alone is not the primary cause of berry aneurysms."}]},{"n":94,"q":"A 65-year-old, male with hypertension develops sudden onset weakness on the right side of the face & arm. His attender recalls history of headache, blurred vision in the patient which leads to the suspicion of increased ICP. Which is the most common cranial nerve involved in raised ICP?","o":[{"t":"Abducens nerve"},{"t":"Facial nerve"},{"t":"Trigeminal nerve"},{"t":"Facial nerve"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Abducens nerve:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The abducens nerve (cranial nerve VI) is most commonly affected in cases of increased ICP."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the ICP increases, it can lead to compression or stretching of the abducens nerve, resulting in weakness or paralysis of the lateral rectus muscle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This causes a sign known as false localizing sign, as the problem is due to the stretching of the nerve."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Trochlear nerve: Increased ICP can potentially affect the trochlear nerve, it is not the most common cranial nerve involved in raised ICP. Therefore, option B is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Trigeminal nerve: Increased ICP can cause headache and facial pain, the trigeminal nerve itself is not typically affected as the primary cranial nerve involved in raised ICP. Therefore, option C is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Facial nerve: Increased ICP can potentially affect the facial nerve, it is not the most common cranial nerve involved in raised ICP."}]},{"n":95,"q":"What is the recommended method for preventing meningococcal meningitis during pregnancy?","o":[{"t":"Ceftriaxone"},{"t":"Rifampicin"},{"t":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"t":"Penicillin G"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Ceftriaxone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ceftriaxone is commonly used for the prophylaxis of meningococcal meningitis in pregnancy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is given as a single intramuscular or intravenous dose for prophylaxis in individuals who have had close contact with a person diagnosed with meningococcal disease. Therefore, option A is correct."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Rifampicin: Rifampicin is also used for the prophylaxis of meningococcal meningitis; however, it is not the preferred choice in pregnancy. Therefore, option B is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Ciprofloxacin: Ciprofloxacin is avoided in pregnancy due to potential adverse effects on the developing fetus. Therefore, option C is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Penicillin G: Penicillin G is effective against many bacterial infections, but it is not the recommended prophylactic treatment for meningococcal meningitis. Therefore, option D is incorrect."}]},{"n":96,"q":"Which of the following symptoms is absent in Horner syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Mydriasis"},{"t":"Ptosis"},{"t":"Anhidrosis"},{"t":"Enophthalmos"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Mydriasis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mydriasis (dilated pupil): This is incorrect. Horner syndrome is characterized by meiosis (constricted pupil) rather than mydriasis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B, C, D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These symptoms are associated with horner syndrome"}]},{"n":97,"q":"Tigroid white matter on MR imaging is seen in which of the following conditions?","o":[{"t":"Pantothenate kinase deficiency"},{"t":"Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease"},{"t":"Neuroferritinopathy"},{"t":"Aceruloplasminemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Pelizaeus-Merzbacher disease is a genetic disorder characterized by abnormal myelination of the central nervous system. The tigroid white matter appearance on MR imaging is a classic finding in this condition due to alternating areas of myelination and demyelination"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Pantothenate kinase deficiency is a rare neurodegenerative disorder characterized by impaired synthesis of coenzyme A (CoA). It is not specifically associated with a tigroid white matter pattern on MR imaging."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Neuroferritinopathy is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder characterized by abnormal iron deposition in the brain. It typically does not present with a tigroid white matter pattern on MR imaging."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Aceruloplasminemia is a rare genetic disorder characterized by the absence or dysfunction of ceruloplasmin, leading to abnormal iron metabolism. It is not specifically associated with a tigroid white matter pattern on MR imaging."}]},{"n":98,"q":"A 52-year-old man was brought to the OPD with complaints of multiple hyperpigmented skin macules which started in his childhood. These fibromas near the eyelid have increased in size causing visual disturbance. Identify the disease shown in the below image.","o":[{"t":"Facial nerve palsy"},{"t":"Plexiform neurofibromatosis"},{"t":"Masticator space abscess"},{"t":"Fibrous dysplasia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Plexiform neurofibromatosis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Plexiform neurofibromatosis is a subtype of neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1), a genetic disorder characterized by the development of neurofibromas, which are benign tumors arising from nerve tissue. Plexiform neurofibromas involve multiple nerves and can cause disfigurement and functional impairments."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Facial nerve palsy: Facial nerve palsy refers to the weakness or paralysis of the muscles innervated by the facial nerve."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Masticator space abscess: Masticator space abscess refers to an abscess (collection of pus) within the masticator space, which is an anatomical region containing the muscles of mastication (chewing)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Fibrous dysplasia: Fibrous dysplasia is a bone disorder characterized by the abnormal development of fibrous tissue within the bone, leading to weakening and deformity."}],"qi":[8547]},{"n":99,"q":"Which of the following is not one of the key clinical features used to diagnose Lewy body dementia?","o":[{"t":"Fluctuating cognition"},{"t":"Recurrent visual hallucinations"},{"t":"REM sleep behaviour disorder"},{"t":"Orthostatic hypotension"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Orthostatic hypotension:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option is correct. Orthostatic hypotension, which refers to a drop in blood pressure upon standing, is not a core clinical feature of LBD. While some individuals with LBD may experience orthostatic hypotension, it is not specific to the diagnosis and can be seen in other neurological or cardiovascular conditions as well."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Fluctuating cognition: This is a core clinical feature of Lewy body dementia (LBD). Patients with LBD commonly experience variations in cognitive function, such as fluctuations in attention, alertness, and ability to process information."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Recurrent visual hallucinations: This is a core clinical feature of LBD. Visual hallucinations, often vivid and detailed, are a common symptom in LBD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD): This is a core clinical feature of LBD and considered prodromal symptom. RBD refers to a disturbance during REM sleep where individuals physically act out their dreams, sometimes leading to injury or harm to themselves or their bed partners."}]},{"n":100,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a patient experiencing repeated inflammation of the optic nerve in both eyes along with widespread inflammation of the spinal cord, and exhibiting visual acuity of 6/60 in the right eye and 6/18 in the left eye, with only a partial response to steroid treatment?","o":[{"t":"Neuromyelitis optica"},{"t":"Subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord"},{"t":"Posterior cerebral artery stroke"},{"t":"Neurosyphilis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Neuromyelitis optica:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NMO is a rare autoimmune disorder, also known as Devic’s disease, characterized by bilateral optic neuritis and rapidly progresses to blindness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other clinical characteristics include Area postrema syndrome, Brainstem syndrome, Cerebral syndrome, Diencephalic syndrome, Narcolepsy, and longitudinal extensive transverse myelitis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord: This option is incorrect. Subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord is typically associated with vitamin B12 deficiency, leading to neurological symptoms such as sensory disturbances, difficulty walking, and spasticity. Option C - Posterior cerebral artery stroke: This option is incorrect. Posterior cerebral artery stroke refers to an ischemic stroke affecting the posterior cerebral artery territory of the brain."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Neurosyphilis: This option is incorrect. Neurosyphilis can present with various neurological symptoms, but recurrent optic neuritis and transverse myelitis are not typical features of neurosyphilis."}]},{"n":101,"q":"A 40-year-old was brought ro OPD with rapidly progressing dementia and myoclonus. Below is the EEG of the patient. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},{"t":"Creuzfeldt-Jakob disease"},{"t":"Generalized tonic clonic seizures"},{"t":"Herpes simplex encephalitis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Creuzfeldt-Jakob disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a rare degenerative neurological disorder. The EEG pattern in CJD often shows characteristic findings, including periodic sharp wave complexes (PSWCs) and generalized slowing, which are consistent with the given EEG pattern."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Periodic sharp wave complexes are high voltage burst every few seconds, going upward only."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - The EEG pattern in hepatic encephalopathy typically shows diffuse slowing rather than the specific pattern seen in the given EEG."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Generalized tonic-clonic seizures (also known as grand mal seizures) typically manifest with a EEG pattern characterized by generalized spike and wave discharges during the seizure activity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - The EEG findings in herpes simplex encephalitis may show focal or multifocal abnormalities, such as periodic lateralized epileptiform discharges (PLEDs) or focal slowing, which are different from the EEG pattern observed in the given image."}],"qi":[8552]},{"n":102,"q":"What is the correct statement about the condition of a 32-year-old woman who visits the clinic with complaints of frequent falls and walking difficulties? The patient has a history of smoking and type-1 diabetes but no history of hypertension or coronary artery disease. During examination, the patient exhibits weakness in both lower limbs, increased reflexes and muscle tone, as well as an unsteady gait. Furthermore, there is a decrease in proprioception and vibration sense in both lower limbs.","o":[{"t":"Only the dorsal column is involved."},{"t":"All patients with a neurological impairment will show macrocytosis."},{"t":"Vitamin B12 supplements do not improve the condition."},{"t":"Copper deficiency produces similar symptoms."}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Copper deficiency produces similar symptoms:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Copper deficiency produces similar symptoms: The patient's clinical presentation is consistent with subacute combined degeneration (SCD) of the spinal cord, which is commonly caused by vitamin B12 deficiency. SCD primarily affects the posterior (dorsal) and lateral columns of the spinal cord. Vitamin B12 supplements are the mainstay of treatment for vitamin B12 deficiency, and they can improve the patient's condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Only the dorsal column is involved: This statement is incorrect because in subacute combined degeneration (SCD) of the spinal cord, both the posterior (dorsal) and lateral columns are involved."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - All patients with a neurological impairment will show macrocytosis: This statement is incorrect. While macrocytosis (enlarged red blood cells) can be seen in some cases of vitamin B12 deficiency, it is not a universal finding. Additionally, not all neurological impairments are associated with macrocytosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Vitamin B12 supplements do not improve the condition: This statement is incorrect. Vitamin B12 supplements are the standard treatment for vitamin B12 deficiency, including subacute combined degeneration (SCD) of the spinal cord."}]},{"n":103,"q":"What is the most probable cause for a 22-year-old man who has a high-grade fever, a purpuric rash, and a CSF sample showing gram-negative diplococci?","o":[{"t":"Neisseria meningitidis"},{"t":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa"},{"t":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},{"t":"E.coli"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A- Neisseria meningitidis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above image is that of a purpuric rash which is seen in meningococcemia caused by Neisseria meningitides. Neisseria meningitidis is a gram negative diplococci. Fulminant meningococcemia presents with a very high fever and hemorrhagic rash."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B- Pseudomonas aeruginosa: Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a gram-negative rod-shaped bacteria."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C- Streptococcus pneumoniae: Streptococcus pneumoniae is a gram-positive, spherical bacteria."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D- E. Coli: E. Coli is a gram-negative rod-shaped bacteria."}],"qi":[8555]},{"n":104,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a 72-year-old former pilot who visits a neurologist, accompanied by his wife, exhibiting symptoms of rapidly advancing dementia, myoclonus, ataxia, and visual impairments? The provided FLAIR MRI scans can assist in confirming the diagnosis.","o":[{"t":"Alzheimer's disease"},{"t":"Vascular dementia"},{"t":"Sporadic CJD"},{"t":"Variant CJD"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D: Variant CJD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above-given case, there is rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus, ataxia, and visual disturbances. FLAIR MRI scans show a hyperintense signal involving the pulvinar and dorsomedial thalamic nuclei bilaterally and are known as the hockey stick sign, which is diagnostic of variant CJD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: In the initial stages, the MRI is normal in Alzheimer’s, and in later stages shows temporal and parietal atrophy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: There is no history of stroke; hence vascular dementia is unlikely to be the diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: The hockey stick sign is not seen in sporadic CJD."}],"qi":[8561]},{"n":105,"q":"A 43-year-old patient comes to the OPD with complains tingling sensation in the hands and feet. Reversible dementia is seen in?","o":[{"t":"SACD"},{"t":"AD"},{"t":"CJD"},{"t":"Pick's disease"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - SACD:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subacute Combined Degeneration (SACD) is a neurological condition characterized by the degeneration of the spinal cord, peripheral nerves, and sometimes the brain primarily due to Vitamin B12 deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical features include reversible dementia, paresthesias in hand and feet, loss of vibration and position sensation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment includes 1000 microgram of Vitamin B12 IM."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - AD (Alzheimer's Disease): Alzheimer's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by irreversible memory loss, cognitive decline, and behavioral changes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - CJD (Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease): Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease leads to rapid neurological decline, including dementia, and unfortunately, it is a fatal condition. CJD is not reversible."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pick's Disease: Pick's disease, also known as frontotemporal dementia, leads to progressive cognitive decline, personality changes, and behavioral disturbances."}]},{"n":106,"q":"Which of the statements below is NOT accurate regarding Parkinson's disease?","o":[{"t":"Manganese can cause secondary Parkinsonism"},{"t":"Usage of Carbidopa leads to on and off phenomenon"},{"t":"Slight tremor in the hand or foot followed by jaw"},{"t":"Cog wheel rigidity or lead pipe rigidity is seen"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Usage of Carbidopa leads to on and off phenomenon:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The \"on and off phenomenon\" refers to fluctuations in motor response, where individuals with Parkinson's disease experience periods of improved motor function (on) followed by periods of reduced or absent response to medication (off)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This phenomenon is more commonly associated with fluctuations in the effectiveness of levodopa itself, rather than carbidopa."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Manganese can cause secondary Parkinsonism: Exposure to high levels of manganese, such as through occupational or environmental sources, can lead to a form of parkinsonism known as manganism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Slight tremor in the hand or foot followed by jaw: One of the cardinal symptoms of Parkinson's disease is a resting tremor, typically starting in the hand or foot. The tremor can progress to involve other body parts, including the jaw."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Cogwheel rigidity or lead pipe rigidity is seen: Rigidity is a characteristic feature of Parkinson's disease. Cogwheel rigidity refers to a jerky resistance to passive movement characterized by a series of brief interruptions, while lead pipe rigidity refers to a continuous resistance throughout the range of movement."}]},{"n":107,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a patient who exhibits symptoms of increased urination, nighttime urination, and involuntary urination, along with a recorded 24-hour urine volume of 7 liters and a urine osmolarity of 260 mOsm/L? The ADH assay shows a recorded value of 0.8 pg/ml, and an MRI of the brain reveals the absence of a bright spot on T1 weighted imaging.","o":[{"t":"Nephrogenic DI"},{"t":"Primary polydipsia"},{"t":"Pituitary DI"},{"t":"Mannitol infusion"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C: Pituitary DI"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case is most suggestive of pituitary diabetes insipidus. There is polyuria and polydipsia. The absence of a pituitary bright spot is suggestive of central DI."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: In nephrogenic DI, the ADH levels are >1pg/ml."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: In primary polydipsia, a pituitary bright spot will be observed."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D : In mannitol infusion, the urine osmolality is >300 mOsm/L."}]},{"n":108,"q":"As an intern in the neurology ward of the Department of Internal Medicine, you encounter a 48-year-old female patient who has been admitted due to muscle weakness. After conducting investigations and examinations, it is found that her nerve conduction shows a decremental response , but she displays signs of flaccidity. Based on these findings, what possible diagnosis would you consider?","o":[{"t":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"t":"GBS"},{"t":"Transverse myelitis"},{"t":"Traumatic neuritis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Myasthenia gravis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myasthenia gravis can present with normal nerve conduction studies and a flaccid weakness pattern. In myasthenia gravis, weakness often worsens with exertion and improves with rest."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Repetitive nerve stimulation test may show decremental response if the muscle is affected (>10% decrease at the frequency of 3 Hz stimulation)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - (GBS - Guillain-Barré syndrome) is incorrect because GBS is characterized by reduced or absent deep tendon reflexes and can show abnormalities in nerve conduction studies."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - (Transverse myelitis) is incorrect because transverse myelitis is typically associated with abnormalities in nerve conduction studies and may present with spastic weakness rather than flaccid weakness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - (Traumatic neuritis) is incorrect because traumatic neuritis would not typically present with flaccid weakness and normal nerve conduction studies."}]},{"n":109,"q":"Extensor Reflex on pinching gastrocnemius muscle is called?","o":[{"t":"Gower Sign"},{"t":"Homan Sign"},{"t":"Oppenheim Sign"},{"t":"Gordon Sign"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Gordon Sign:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The extensor reflex on pinching the gastrocnemius muscle is known as the Gordon sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is an abnormal reflex response where pinching the gastrocnemius muscle leads to extension of the big toe instead of the normal flexion response. The Gordon sign can be seen in upper motor neuron lesions, such as in pyramidal tract dysfunction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - (Gower Sign) is incorrect because the Gower sign is associated with Duchenne muscular dystrophy and refers to the characteristic way that individuals with muscle weakness in the lower extremities rise from a squatting or sitting position. They use their hands to \"walk\" up their legs and push themselves into a standing position."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - (Homan Sign) is incorrect because the Homan sign is a clinical finding associated with deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in the lower extremities. It is characterized by pain in the calf upon passive dorsiflexion of the foot."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - (Oppenheim Sign) is incorrect because the Oppenheim sign is a test used to assess upper motor neuron dysfunction. It involves applying firm pressure along the anterior tibial surface from the knee to the ankle, and a positive sign is indicated by a downward movement of the big toe."}]},{"n":110,"q":"A patient was asked to copy the face of a clock and his drawing is shown below. What is the correct term to be used here?","o":[{"t":"Confabulation"},{"t":"Hemispatial neglect"},{"t":"Kinetic apraxia"},{"t":"Asterixis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Hemispatial neglect:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hemispatial neglect is the inability to report, respond, and orient to stimuli in one-half of the space despite having no motor or sensory deficits."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes of spatial neglect include stroke, traumatic brain injury, brain tumors, and aneurysms. Rarely, neurodegenerative diseases can cause neglect symptoms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Confabulation is a phenomenon in which the patient fills in memory gaps with made up stories and inaccurate facts."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Kinetic apraxia is the inability to perform a fine movement or task despite having the desire and physical capability to do it. The lesion lies in the paracentral cortex."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"OptionD - Asterixis is a type of negative myoclonus where irregular lapses of the posture of body parts is noted."}],"qi":[8570]},{"n":111,"q":"Which of the following statements about juvenile myoclonic epilepsy is false? Polygenic inheritance Lamotrigine helps in controlling myoclonic jerks Benzodiazepines alone can control myoclonic jerks Valproate should be avoided","o":[{"t":"1,2 & 3 are correct"},{"t":"1&3 are correct"},{"t":"2& 4 are correct"},{"t":"1,2,3 and 4 are correct"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - (1, 2, and 3 are correct):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option correctly states that statements 1, 2, and 3 are true, while statement 4 is incorrect."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect statement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Valproate should be avoided: This statement is incorrect. Valproate is used in the treatment of JMS and should be taken lifelong."}]},{"n":112,"q":"Which of the following is the correct statement regarding Huntington’s chorea?","o":[{"t":"It is a trinucleotide repeat disorder"},{"t":"There is a loss of function type of mutation"},{"t":"There are abnormal repeats of CUG"},{"t":"Abnormality is seen due to mutation in chromosome 6"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - It is a trinucleotide repeat disorder:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Huntington's chorea, also known as Huntington's disease, is an autosomal inherited disease that occurs at an early age in the next generation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Huntington's chorea is a trinucleotide repeat disorder involving the expansion of a repeated trinucleotide sequence (CAG) within the HTT gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - There is a loss of function type of mutation: Huntington's chorea is characterized by a gain-of-function mutation caused by the expansion of the CAG trinucleotide repeat."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - There are abnormal repeats of CUG: Huntington's chorea is a trinucleotide repeat disorder involving the expansion of a repeated trinucleotide sequence (CAG) within the HTT gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Abnormality is seen due to mutation in chromosome 6: Huntington's chorea is caused by a specific mutation in the huntingtin (HTT) gene, which is located on chromosome 4."}]},{"n":113,"q":"A patient with intentional tremor has a problem doing the heel-to-shin test and repeatedly misses while doing the finger-nose test. What is the site of the lesion in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Cerebellum"},{"t":"Caudate"},{"t":"Globus pallidus"},{"t":"Subthalamic nucleus"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Cerebellum:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coordination refers to the orchestration and fluidity of movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coordination relies on the normal functioning of the cerebellar and basal ganglia systems."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Finger-to-nose testing is primarily a test of cerebellar function. The Patient is asked to touch his or her index finger repetitively to the nose and then to the examiner’s outstretched hand, which moves with each repetition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heel-to-shin test is a similar test for the lower limb; in the supine position, the patient is asked to slide the heel of each foot from the knee down the shin of the other leg."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B, C, D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caudate nuclei, Globus pallidus, and Subthalamic nucleus are components of the basal ganglia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lesions in this region present with gait abnormalities, lack of motor symmetry, and voluntary movements of the body"}]},{"n":114,"q":"A 30-year-old lady has unilateral ptosis and exhibits worsening symptoms by evening. She was diagnosed with myasthenia gravis. What is the drug of choice in this case?","o":[{"t":"Pyridostigmine"},{"t":"Prednisolone"},{"t":"Cyclosporine"},{"t":"Plasma exchange"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Pyridostigmine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The goals of treatment are to maximise the activity of acetylcholine at remaining receptors in the neuromuscular junctions and to limit or abolish the immunological attack on motor end plates."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The duration of action of acetylcholine is prolonged by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pyridostigmine is the most widely used anticholinesterase drug and it is initiated at a dosage of 30-60 mg 3-4 times daily."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The beneficial action of oral pyridostigmine begins with 15-30 mins and lasts for 3-4 hrs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The maximum useful dose of pyridostigmine rarely exceeds more than 300 mg daily."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Overdosage with anticholinesterase drugs may cause increased weakness and other side effects"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The goals of treatment are to maximise the activity of acetylcholine at remaining receptors in the neuromuscular junctions and to limit or abolish the immunological attack on motor end plates."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The duration of action of acetylcholine is prolonged by inhibiting acetylcholinesterase."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Prednisolone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prednisolone is used as a single-dose drug to improve the symptoms of myasthenia gravis patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cyclosporine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cyclosporine may be effective in myasthenia gravis, but they are associated with severe side effects like hypertension and nephrotoxicity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Plasma exchange:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Plasma exchange has been used therapeutically in MG and it is mainly used during a difficult period or before surgery."}]},{"n":115,"q":"Seizures are seen in what percentage of RTA patients with brain contusion/loss of consciousness?","o":[{"t":"Development of seizures in 17%"},{"t":"Development of seizures in 45%"},{"t":"Development of seizures in 75%"},{"t":"No relation between trauma and seizures."}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Development of seizures in 17%:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Convulsions are uncommon immediately after TBI, but a brief period of tonic extensor posturing or a few clonic movements of the limbs just after the moment of impact can occur."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It has been estimated that 17% of individuals with brain contusion, subdural hematoma, or prolonged LOC will develop seizure disorder."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Penetrating injuries have a much higher rate of subsequent epilepsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post-head injury, phenytoin is used in the treatment of seizures."}]},{"n":116,"q":"A patient had features of resting tremors, bradykinesia, and postural instability. He became bedridden and died due to respiratory failure. A brain biopsy is likely to show which of the following?","o":[{"t":"Lewy bodies"},{"t":"Hirano bodies"},{"t":"Guarneri bodies"},{"t":"Negri bodies"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lewy bodies:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A patient with resting tremors, bradykinesia, and postural instability indicates a diagnosis of Parkinson's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The brain biopsy of a Parkinson’s patient shows Lewy bodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lewy body is an eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusion that contains both neurofilaments and a -synuclein"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hallmark features of Parkinson’s disease Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta Reduced striatal dopamine. Intraneuronal proteinaceous inclusions are known as Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced striatal dopamine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intraneuronal proteinaceous inclusions are known as Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduced striatal dopamine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intraneuronal proteinaceous inclusions are known as Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hirano bodies:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hirano bodies are observed in ALS and Parkinsonism-dementia complex."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Guarneri bodies:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Guarneri bodies are found in cells infected with smallpox or vaccinia virus"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Negri bodies:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Negri bodies are eosinophilic cytoplasmic inclusions in brain neurons that are composed of rabies virus proteins and viral RNA"}]},{"n":117,"q":"An 8-year-old child with a history of GTCS came with an episode of convulsions for more than 45 minutes. What will be the appropriate management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Lorazepam followed by levetiracetam"},{"t":"Valproate followed by gabapentin"},{"t":"Carbamazepine followed by lorazepam"},{"t":"Levetiracetam followed by valproate"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lorazepam followed by levetiracetam:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Status epilepticus refers to continuous seizures or repetitive, discrete seizures with impaired consciousness in the interictal period."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For impending and early SE, the appropriate treatment is Inj lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg is administered followed by levetiracetam 20-30 mg/kg."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For established and early refractory SE, Propofol 2 mg/kg IV followed by 2-10 mg/kg/hr with the addition of anticonvulsants like levetiracetam."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Valproate followed by gabapentin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Valproate is used in the treatment of GTCS"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Carbamazepine followed by lorazepam:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Carbamazepine is used in the treatment of focal seizures."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Levetiracetam followed by valproate:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Levetiracetam followed by valproate is used in the treatment of GTCS"}]},{"n":118,"q":"A child is brought with a 2-week history of not feeding well and excessive irritability. History of TB for sister is present and is taking treatment for it. In workup, LP was performed. Which of the following is likely to be found in CSF?","o":[{"t":"Lymphocytosis, low sugar, and high protein"},{"t":"Lymphocytosis, high sugar, and high protein"},{"t":"Neutrophils, low sugar and low protein"},{"t":"Neutrophils, low sugar and high protein"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lymphocytosis, low sugar and high protein:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option is the most likely finding in the CSF of a child with a suspected tuberculous meningitis, which is caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The history of TB in the sister raises the suspicion of an infectious etiology."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Examination of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) reveals a high leukocyte count (up to 1000/mL), usually with a predominance of lymphocytes but sometimes with a predominance of neutrophils in the early stage; a protein content of 1–8 g/L (100–800 mg/dL); and a low glucose concentration."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Lymphocytosis, high sugar and high protein: While lymphocytosis and high protein levels are expected in a CNS infection, high sugar levels (hyperglycorrhachia) are not typical. Viruses and certain bacteria tend to decrease CSF glucose levels due to increased consumption. Therefore, this option is less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Neutrophils, low sugar, and low protein: Neutrophils are a type of white blood cell associated with bacterial infections. The combination of low sugar and low protein levels is not characteristic of most CNS infections, as infections typically lead to increased protein levels due to the inflammatory response. This pattern is not consistent with the clinical presentation and history provided."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Neutrophils, low sugar, and high protein: While neutrophils could be present in the CSF in cases of acute bacterial meningitis, the combination of low sugar and high protein levels is not typical. Acute bacterial infections tend to lower CSF glucose levels due to consumption, but they also lead to an increase in protein levels. This option does not match the expected pattern for the given clinical scenario."}]},{"n":119,"q":"A 7-year-old child from a malaria-endemic region presents with fever, altered mental status, seizures, and coma. Physical examination reveals no focal neurological deficits. Blood smear shows the presence of Plasmodium falciparum parasites. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Tubercular meningitis"},{"t":"Viral meningitis"},{"t":"Cerebral malaria"},{"t":"Fungal meningitis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cerebral malaria:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s presentation with fever, altered mental status, seizures, and coma with a blood smear shows the presence of Plasmodium falciparum parasites, and his history of living in a malaria-endemic region is suggestive of cerebral malaria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral malaria is the most severe neurological complication of infection with Plasmodium falciparum malaria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients develop fever, headache, body ache, seizures and progressively, delirium and coma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer."}]},{"n":120,"q":"Unilateral pulsatile headache in a woman with photophobia and phonophobia which is exaggerated on eating meals is seen in?","o":[{"t":"Migraine"},{"t":"Tension headache"},{"t":"Cluster headache"},{"t":"Malingering"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Migraine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Unilateral pulsatile headaches with photophobia and phonophobia are the clinical features of migraine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If 4 out of 5 below symptoms are present with ‡5 episodes of migraine per year - Routine case of migraine. (Mneumonic: POUND) P: Pulsatile / Throbbing headache - Nature of the headache. O: One-day illness (After some rest, the patient can perform the usual routine tasks) U: Unilateral headache (can also be bilateral) N: Nausea (In most patients, the smell or sight of food makes them pukish) D: Disabling in character (Very important factor)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P: Pulsatile / Throbbing headache - Nature of the headache."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"O: One-day illness (After some rest, the patient can perform the usual routine tasks)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"U: Unilateral headache (can also be bilateral)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"N: Nausea (In most patients, the smell or sight of food makes them pukish)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D: Disabling in character (Very important factor)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P: Pulsatile / Throbbing headache - Nature of the headache."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"O: One-day illness (After some rest, the patient can perform the usual routine tasks)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"U: Unilateral headache (can also be bilateral)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"N: Nausea (In most patients, the smell or sight of food makes them pukish)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D: Disabling in character (Very important factor)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Tension headache:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tension headache presents with band-like compression over the head at the forehead/Occipital area."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cluster headache:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cluster headache presents with retro-orbital pain and epiphora."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Malingering:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above symptoms indicate migraine."}]}]);
